\begin{abstract}
Realizing practical quantum computers requires quantum error correction, but the most widely implemented approach, the planar surface code, demands a substantial physical qubit overhead. 
Here, we demonstrate that partitioning quantum processors into manageable,
flat modules with non-local inter-module connections provides a natural
solution.
By wiring sparse, static boundary connections between Euclidean planar modules, we construct modular hyperbolic surface and color codes, based on new families of semi-hyperbolic codes. 
Using modular syndrome extraction circuits and efficient neural network and matching decoders, our circuit-level simulations show that this modular memory design can reduce the physical qubit overhead by tenfold or more compared to the surface code, even under elevated inter-module seam error rates. 
Projecting to larger system sizes, we find modular codes with encoding rate $k/n=1/16$ and distance $d\geq 22$, exceeding both the rate and distance of the $[[288,12,18]]$ two-gross bivariate bicycle code while using mostly nearest-neighbor gates within planar modules, and implying an over $30\times$ overhead reduction relative to surface codes.
We develop fault-tolerant walking circuits for implementing logic via code automorphisms, which we co-design with low-weight, highly symmetrical logical bases and a modular extractor system that is $\approx 4.5\times$ smaller than the code itself.
Combining these methods, our modular memory can be used as dense storage in a universal fault-tolerant architecture, unlocking the efficiency of high-rate codes without sacrificing the fabrication advantages of planar modules.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

Quantum computers can solve certain computational problems exponentially faster than any known classical algorithm \cite{deutsch_jozsa,shor}, with anticipated applications in quantum simulation for material science, drug design and other areas \cite{lloyd,quantum_chemistry_review}, while also presenting significant challenges to current cryptographic infrastructure \cite{shor}. However, realizing these applications requires quantum error correction (QEC), in which many physical qubits are encoded into logical qubits to protect information from inevitable noise and imperfections in the hardware. 

The surface code has led experimental efforts in this direction, owing to its compatibility with a square-grid layout on a planar chip, and recently enabled the first experimental demonstrations of exponential suppression of the logical error rate at physical error rates below threshold~\cite{surface_code_below_threshold}. 
However, the surface code requires a substantial resource overhead to achieve logical error rates relevant to practical applications, with utility-scale fault-tolerant quantum computers expected to require around 100k to 1 million qubits~\cite{gidney2025factor,low2026denserplanarsurfacecode}.
Furthermore, fabricating a monolithic chip at this scale becomes impractical, due to fundamental constraints on fabrication, yield and coupler density (see \Cref{fig:hardware_model}a), motivating modular architectures in which smaller units are networked together \cite{nickerson2013topological, ramette2024fault, strikis_2023}.

\begin{figure*}
    \centering

    \caption{Hardware models. In all panels, black circles represent data qubits and red (blue) squares represent measure qubits for $X$ ($Z$) stabilizers. \textbf{a}, A monolithic grid architecture, in which qubits are placed on a plane with 2D nearest-neighbor grid connectivity. This geometry supports the Euclidean surface code, shown here. \textbf{b}, A general QLDPC architecture, in which each qubit is coupled to a constant number of other qubits, without additional constraints on geometric locality. Couplers may cross and may be long-range, as shown here for the Gross code~\cite{bivariate_bicycle_codes}. Each edge represents a coupler between qubits. \textbf{c},~A boundary-connected modular architecture. Planar modules are connected by flexible inter-module couplers routed without geometric constraints. The wiring pattern realizes a closed hyperbolic surface with genus $g\geq2$. The inset shows the planar connectivity within a single module.}
    \label{fig:hardware_model}
\end{figure*}

An alternative path to fault-tolerant quantum computation with lower overhead lies in the use of more general, high-rate quantum low-density parity-check (QLDPC) codes~\cite{breuckmanqldpc}.
In principle these codes can substantially reduce resource overheads relative to the surface code~\cite{bivariate_bicycle_codes}, however their implementation in solid state systems requires a high density of long-range crossing couplers (see \Cref{fig:hardware_model}b).

Here we demonstrate that the practical necessity of partitioning a quantum computer into modules can be leveraged to dramatically reduce the resource requirements of QEC.
We show that high encoding rates can be achieved using flat Euclidean modules of around 100 qubits, with sparse and fixed non-local couplers connecting qubits at module boundaries (see \Cref{fig:hardware_model}c).
This connectivity is motivated by state-of-the-art superconducting quantum computers, for which local planar gates now operate below the surface code threshold~\cite{surface_code_below_threshold}, while link resonators connecting chips through cables have been demonstrated to achieve error rates approaching 1\% \cite{Zhong_2021, Niu_2023, Qiu_2025}.

Our constructions are derived from color codes and surface codes on high-genus hyperbolic surfaces~\cite{freedman2002z2,breuckmann_2016_constructions,delfosse_tradeoffs,Breuckmann_2017_semihyperbolic,higgott_2024_floquet}.
Each module forms a locally flat Euclidean tile of the overall QLDPC code.
These tiles are connected using inter-module couplers to form the global connectivity of the hyperbolic tiling~\cite{higgott_2024_floquet,sutcliffe_2025_distributed}.
We design modular syndrome extraction circuits for these codes that are tailored to our distributed hardware model and develop efficient and accurate machine learning \cite{bausch2024learning, senior2025scalablerealtimeneuraldecoder} and matching decoders \cite{Higgott2025sparseblossom}.
Our circuit-level simulations demonstrate a $10\times$ reduction in physical qubit
overhead compared to the surface code at a logical error rate of $10^{-10}$,
assuming physical error rates expected for near-term experiments (SI1000 noise model with $p=0.1\%$, see Methods)
and inter-module gates that are ten times noisier ($p_{seam}=1\%$).
This is achieved with a modular hyperbolic surface code of 120 modules of 116 qubits each
(see \Cref{fig:frontier_codes_plots}), using multi-rate syndrome extraction circuits
that show substantial tolerance to slow and noisy inter-module couplers.
Projecting to larger system sizes, we find these savings scale to an over $30\times$
overhead reduction, requiring only 36 physical qubits per logical qubit,
including measure qubits, to achieve a distance of 20--24.
This is both lower overhead and higher distance than the [[288,12,18]] two-gross bivariate bicycle code (48 physical qubits per logical qubit, including check qubits)~\cite{bivariate_bicycle_codes,yoder2025tour}, despite requiring much simpler connectivity that is mostly local in 2D.
By comparison, a Euclidean surface code at the same distance requires over 1000
physical qubits per logical qubit.
Previous work using 2D nearest-neighbor gates~\cite{yoked_surface_code,low2026denserplanarsurfacecode,geher2025directional,gu2026nearest,nixon2026vine} or hyperbolic codes~\cite{higgott_2021_subsystem,higgott_2024_floquet,flag_proxy_hyperbolic_color} has demonstrated qubit overhead reductions of up to around $5\times$ at the circuit level.

Furthermore, we show how our modular QLDPC codes can be augmented to an architecture capable of implementing universal fault-tolerant computation.
We design fault-tolerant circuits for implementing logical gates via code automorphisms, corresponding to symmetries of the hyperbolic tiling that permute the modules, using only nearest-neighbor gates within each module and one additional seam of inter-module couplers on each side of a module.
We further design low-weight, highly symmetrical logical bases, as well as modular extractor systems that can measure logical Pauli operators via code surgery.
These techniques are combined to enable a single extractor system to access hundreds of distinct logical Pauli operators, allowing logical qubits to be teleported into and out of the code, and enabling universal computation with low space overhead.
All of these methods can be implemented using hardware components that have already been demonstrated with fidelity very close to those assumed here \cite{surface_code_below_threshold,Zhong_2021, Niu_2023, Qiu_2025}. 

\section{Modular hyperbolic codes}

\begin{figure*}
    \centering

    \caption{Construction of a modular hyperbolic color code, using the $[[256,8,12]]$ Bolza color code as an example. \textbf{a}, The base infinite hyperbolic tiling, shown in the Poincar\'e disk model, has triangular faces with 8 meeting at each vertex (face configuration V8.8.8). Its vertices are three-colorable (red, green, blue), with one vertex of each color per triangle. Each edge is assigned the third color, absent from its two vertices. The fundamental polygon of the genus-2 Bolza surface is shaded, and contains 16 triangles. \textbf{b}, The semi-hyperbolic color code obtained by replacing each triangular face with a Euclidean hexagonal tiling of side length $L=4$, giving a tiling of the surface by hexagons and octagons. A data qubit is associated with each vertex, and both an $X$ and a $Z$ stabilizer with each face. Sides of the fundamental polygon that are identified carry the same label. The code is partitioned into 8 modules, each derived from a pair of triangles of the base tiling sharing an edge of a chosen color (here red edges). \textbf{c}, The two modules outlined in \textbf{b} shown in detail. Two measure qubits are placed in each hexagonal face within a module, with additional measure qubits on the boundary. Qubits within a module are coupled by nearest-neighbor in-module couplers on a 2D grid (solid lines), while stabilizers spanning multiple modules are measured using inter-module couplers at the boundary (dashed lines).}
    \label{fig:bolza_color_code_overview}
\end{figure*}



Our codes are defined on tilings of closed hyperbolic surfaces, i.e.~closed surfaces of constant negative curvature, which have genus $g\geq2$.
Codes defined on such tilings encode $k$ logical qubits into $n$ physical qubits with a constant encoding rate $k/n$~\cite{freedman2002z2,delfosse_tradeoffs,breuckmann_2016_constructions}.
While other QLDPC code families are known to have constant rate asymptotically~\cite{tillich2014hgp,breuckmann2021product,panteleev2022good}, the rate for hyperbolic codes is unusually high in practice, approaching $1/2$ for color codes on 12.12.12 tilings~\cite{delfosse_tradeoffs,breuckmann_2016_constructions}.
For example, our search finds $[[n,k,d]]=[[648,328,8]]$ and $[[672,172,10]]$ hyperbolic color codes (see \Cref{tab:candidates_L1_color}), where $d$ is the code distance, which have rates comparable to recently proposed ultra-high-rate QLDPC codes~\cite{Kasai2026breaking,zhao2026ultrahighratequantumerrorcorrection}.
Although their distance grows logarithmically with $n$, for practical system sizes it is only a factor of two to three below the distances of around 20--24 needed for utility-scale quantum computation.

We close this gap by fine-graining: we replace each face of the base hyperbolic tiling with a patch of a Euclidean lattice of side length $L$ (with $L=1$ recovering the base tiling) to obtain a \emph{semi-hyperbolic} tiling, from which we construct a new family of semi-hyperbolic surface codes, distinct from those of Ref.~\cite{Breuckmann_2017_semihyperbolic}, and, for the first time, semi-hyperbolic color codes.
Fine-graining leaves $k$ unchanged while increasing $d$ by a factor of roughly $L$ and $n$ by a factor of roughly $L^2$, trading encoding rate for distance while approximately preserving the figure of merit $kd^2/n$.
Since the base hyperbolic codes have such a high rate, the resulting codes retain high encoding rates (as high as $k/n=1/16$ among the codes we find) at large distances ($d\geq22$).

Fine-graining also flattens the tiling locally, allowing the semi-hyperbolic code to be partitioned into Euclidean planar modules.
Semi-hyperbolic Floquet codes on the same class of tilings, including their modular implementation, have previously been studied~\cite{higgott_2024_floquet,sutcliffe_2025_distributed} (the latter using a generic graph-partitioning heuristic), but color codes have not previously been constructed on them.
We refer to the resulting modular semi-hyperbolic codes simply as \emph{modular hyperbolic} codes, and find many new examples by searching over suitable hyperbolic tilings, as described below.

A modular hyperbolic \textit{color} code is derived from a tiling of a hyperbolic surface with triangular faces.
An example of such a tiling of the infinite hyperbolic plane with 8 triangles meeting at each vertex is shown in \Cref{fig:bolza_color_code_overview}a. The vertices of the tiling are three-colorable, with one vertex of each color per triangle, and we assign each edge the third color absent from its two vertices.
A tiling of a finite, closed surface can be obtained by cutting a polygon (called the \emph{fundamental polygon}) out of this infinite plane and identifying (i.e.~gluing) pairs of its boundary edges together to fold the flat polygon into a closed manifold.
The fundamental polygon of the genus-2 Bolza surface is shaded in \Cref{fig:bolza_color_code_overview}a, with the identified pairs of edges labeled in \Cref{fig:bolza_color_code_overview}b.
Each triangular face is replaced with a Euclidean hexagonal tiling of side length $L$ to form a semi-hyperbolic tiling of hexagons and octagons~\cite{higgott_2024_floquet}.
An $X$ stabilizer and a $Z$ stabilizer generator are then defined on each face of the resulting three-face-colorable tiling.

We partition a semi-hyperbolic color code into modules by deriving each module from a contiguous set of triangular faces in the base hyperbolic tiling.
In \Cref{fig:bolza_color_code_overview}b-c we show an example of a \textit{quadrilateral} module partition, for which each module is defined by a pair of triangles in the base tiling that share an edge of a chosen color (e.g.~red edges).
By combining the eight triangles together that share a vertex of a given color (e.g.~red vertices), we can also obtain a larger \textit{octagon} module partition. 

We also construct modular hyperbolic \textit{surface} codes, derived instead from a hyperbolic tiling of quadrilateral faces.
Each quadrilateral face is replaced with a square-grid tiling of side length $L$.
Rather than placing data qubits on the edges of this fine-grained tiling~\cite{Breuckmann_2017_semihyperbolic}, we take the dual tiling and bicolor its faces red and blue.
We then associate a data qubit with each vertex of this dual tiling, an $X$ stabilizer with each red face and a $Z$ stabilizer with each blue face (see \Cref{fig:frontier_codes_plots}b), analogous to the rotated surface code.
Compared with placing data qubits on edges, this has two advantages. First, for odd $L$ it distributes the high-weight corner stabilizers between $X$ and $Z$ types (which are related by a symmetry when the tiling is regular, see \Cref{sec:asymmetry_X_Z}), which can help balance the $X$ and $Z$ distances and improve the code parameters. Second, no data qubits lie on the edges of the base quadrilaterals, which allows uniform module partitions.

Logical operators are supported on non-trivial cycles around a handle of the surface, with a genus-$g$ tiling supporting $4g$ logical qubits for the hyperbolic color codes and $2g$ logical qubits for the surface codes \cite{bombin_color,bombin_delgado_toric_surface_color}.
By the Gauss-Bonnet theorem, the genus of a closed hyperbolic surface grows linearly with its area.
Consequently, for a given code family, the number of logical qubits increases linearly with the number of modules, alongside an increasing distance (which grows with the tiling's combinatorial systole).
The negative curvature responsible for this scaling is absent within any module,
each of which is a flat Euclidean patch, and arises entirely from the
inter-module couplers connecting them.

\section{Modular Syndrome Extraction}
\begin{figure*}
    \centering

    \caption{Simulation data for modular hyperbolic codes using an SI1000 noise model with $p=0.1\%$ and inter-module CZ gates adjusted to have a noise strength of $p_{\mathrm{seam}}=1\%$ (see Methods for details). \textbf{a}, Modular hyperbolic color codes derived from V8.8.8 hyperbolic tilings using $L=2$ and an octagon module partition. \textbf{b}, Modular hyperbolic surface codes derived from $\{4,10\}$ regular hyperbolic tilings with $L=5$ and a rectangle module partition. In both panels we compare with Euclidean surface codes partitioned into modules of a comparable size (with additional measure qubits on the boundaries).}
    \label{fig:frontier_codes_plots}
\end{figure*}
We design fault-tolerant syndrome extraction circuits that respect the connectivity constraints of the boundary-connected modular architecture, and benchmark them under a circuit-level noise model with elevated seam noise.
For the modular hyperbolic color codes, we place a pair of measure qubits in each hexagonal plaquette in the bulk and use them to prepare a Bell state.
The Bell state is coupled to the data qubits and used to extract the plaquette's $X$ stabilizer, then measured to obtain a stabilizer measurement along with a flag measurement \cite{chao_reichardt_flag,flag_QEC,takada_keisuke24}.
The process is then repeated in the opposite basis for the $Z$ stabilizers.
Stabilizers that span modules are measured using cat states (Greenberger--Horne--Zeilinger states) supported on $m$ measure qubits, which are prepared using inter-module couplers.
Once prepared, the $m$-qubit cat state is then coupled to the stabilizer's data qubits using in-module gates and then measured to obtain the stabilizer measurement and $m-1$ flag measurements.

The circuits for the modular hyperbolic surface codes are similar, with some important differences.
A single measure qubit (instead of a Bell state) is used to measure each square plaquette stabilizer in the bulk.
The $X$ and $Z$ stabilizers are also interleaved, leading to commutativity constraints.
We find valid low-depth extraction schedules by solving the relevant constraint satisfaction problem on the base tiling and lifting to translationally-invariant schedules within the modules.
Additionally, we design multi-rate circuits that measure the high-weight inter-module stabilizers less frequently (once every three QEC rounds), which we find leads to improved logical error rates while allowing for slower inter-module gates.

To decode efficiently we develop and train a variant of the AlphaQubit decoder (AQ) for the modular hyperbolic color codes~\cite{senior2025scalablerealtimeneuraldecoder}.
For the modular hyperbolic surface codes we decode using a correlated minimum-weight perfect matching (MWPM) decoder~\cite{Higgott2025sparseblossom}.

Our circuit-level simulations are presented in \Cref{fig:frontier_codes_plots}.
For the modular hyperbolic color codes (decoded with AlphaQubit) our simulations show an $8\times$ density improvement when compared to the Euclidean surface code partitioned into modules of a similar size.
By projecting to larger system sizes (details in Methods), we find that the savings increase to an over $30\times$ overhead reduction, with a modular hyperbolic color code requiring only 36 physical qubits per logical to achieve a distance of 20--24 using planar 72-qubit modules (see \Cref{tab:candidates_888}).
For modular hyperbolic surface codes, our simulations demonstrate a $10\times$ reduction in the number of physical qubits per logical qubit needed to reach a logical error rate of $10^{-10}$, increasing to a $15\times$ saving when extrapolating to larger system sizes.
While the modular hyperbolic surface codes are not as dense as their color code counterparts, they have the potential practical advantage of supporting fast and accurate MWPM decoding.
We find that our circuits for both hyperbolic color and surface codes are highly tolerant to noisy inter-module gates, with even $10\times$ higher seam errors than bulk errors leading to only a modest increase in logical error rate (see \Cref{fig:ler_vs_seam_noise}).

\section{Logical Control and Computation}
\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering

    \caption{Logical control architecture: \emph{code automorphisms} and a \emph{ribbon extractor}. \textbf{a}, A symplectic pair of homologically non-trivial cycles on the semi-hyperbolic tiling of the $[[6400,104,30]]$ color code, drawn as solid and dashed lines. The two cycles are related by $\rho$, a global rigid rotation of the tiling by $\pi/2$ (green arrows). Together with a second rotation $\sigma$ (purple arrows), these generate all rotations of the tiling, each of which permutes the modules while preserving the codespace (a code automorphism). Logical Pauli operators are supported on the monochromatic substrings of a cycle, obtained by restricting it to edges of a single color. Two of the three colors (e.g. blue and red) give four independent logical operators per cycle. Operators on the same cycle commute, and anticommuting pairs are supported across the symplectic pair, so each pair defines four logical qubits. For the $[[6400,104,30]]$ code, the orbit of a single seed cycle under these rotations generates a symplectic basis for 100 of the 104 logical qubits. A single extractor then suffices for 100 qubits: it measures the operators on the seed cycle, and every other cycle in the orbit is rotated onto it. \textbf{b}, A ribbon extractor attached to a non-trivial cycle of the memory code, shown in detail in the inset. The extractor is itself boundary-connected and modular, containing ancilla qubits and ancilla checks. It couples to the memory code modules only along the cycle, at the boundaries. It can fault-tolerantly measure all $2^4-1=15$ non-trivial products of the four logical Pauli operators on the cycle, using roughly $22\%$ additional physical qubits relative to the memory.}
    \label{fig:logic}
\end{figure*}

To operate on the logical information stored in the modular hyperbolic memory we combine two ingredients: code surgery and code automorphisms. In code surgery~\cite{cohen2022low-overhead,cross2024improved,Williamson2026,ide2024fault,swaroop2026universal,he2025extractorsqldpcarchitecturesefficient}, a generalization of lattice surgery~\cite{horsman2012surface, landahl2014quantum}, an ancilla system of data and measure qubits is coupled to the memory code in order to measure its logical operators. 
These measurements implement fault-tolerant logic and allow logical information to be moved into and out of the memory~\cite{Breuckmann_2017_semihyperbolic,yoder2025tour}. Code automorphisms are symmetries that permute the physical qubits while preserving the codespace. Here they are global rotations and translations of the hyperbolic tiling, and they allow a single ancilla system to measure many different logical operators.

Combining these two ingredients with low overhead requires co-designing the logical basis of the code with both the surgery ancilla system and the circuits that implement automorphisms.
To teleport an individual logical qubit into and out of the memory, we must be able to measure single-qubit logical $\overline{X}_i$ and $\overline{Z}_i$ operators (see \Cref{sec:logical_measurement_methods}).
These single-qubit logical operators of the code are derived from a \textit{canonical homology basis} of the underlying hyperbolic surface.
On an orientable genus-$g$ surface, a full canonical homology basis consists of $g$ \emph{symplectic pairs} of homologically non-trivial cycles that cross each other an odd number of times (see \Cref{fig:logic}a for one such pair), while cycles from different pairs cross an even number of times.
In a hyperbolic color code, restricting a cycle to edges of one color defines a logical operator, and two colors give four independent, commuting $X$ and $Z$ logical operators per cycle. With anticommuting partners on the other cycle, each symplectic pair encodes four logical qubits~\cite{bombin_color} (two for a hyperbolic surface code), whose operators can be deformed onto module boundaries.
While it is known that a shortest homology basis can be found in polynomial time~\cite{Erickson_Whittlesey}, such a basis does not ensure that cycles form symplectic pairs, nor that they can be mapped onto each other via automorphisms.
To satisfy these additional constraints, we enumerate low-weight homologically non-trivial \emph{seed} cycles and generate the orbit of each seed cycle under the action of the automorphisms we can implement.
We then search for $g^\prime\leq g$ symplectic pairs within the orbit of each single seed loop (Methods).
For the $[[6400,104,30]]$ code ($g=26$) and the $[[3600,104,22]]$ code derived from the same tiling with $L=3$, the orbit of the seed cycle in \Cref{fig:logic}a contains $g^\prime=25$ symplectic pairs, covering 100 of the 104 logical qubits (\Cref{tab:candidates_888}).

We access this seed cycle by attaching a modular \emph{ribbon extractor}, an extractor system~\cite{he2025extractorsqldpcarchitecturesefficient} that we design for boundary-connected modules. The name comes from the fact that this system, which extracts logical information from the code, is a closed loop of non-trivial width attached to a cycle that wraps around the base code. 
Here we focus on ribbon extractors for modular hyperbolic color codes with the quadrilateral module partition; we expect the same methods to apply to other module partitions and to modular hyperbolic surface codes.
Like the memory itself, the extractor is built from planar modules joined at their boundaries, and it attaches to the memory along the cycle that supports the logical operators, using inter-module couplers at module boundaries as in the rest of the architecture. A single extractor can fault-tolerantly measure all $2^4-1=15$ non-trivial products of the four logical Pauli operators on the cycle it is attached to.
We prove (Supplementary Information) that this construction preserves the full distance $d$.
For both the $[[3600,104,22]]$ and $[[6400,104,30]]$ codes, the extractor is about $4.5\times$ smaller than the memory itself.

To measure the other logical operators with the ribbon extractor we implement code automorphisms using fault-tolerant \textit{walking} circuits.
Two rotations $\rho$ and $\sigma$ about neighboring vertices generate all orientation-preserving rotations and translations of the tiling (\Cref{fig:logic}a).
For quadrilateral modules, $\rho$ and $\sigma$ each map every module onto another module with the same internal layout and orientation.
Since the modules are in general not mapped to their neighbors in the code by these global rotations, we add an additional inter-module seam of couplers from the north boundary of each module to the south boundary of its image in $\rho$.
An $O(L)$-step walking circuit of SWAP gates implements the move while all stabilizers originally internal to a module continue to be measured at every step, and simulations of a simplified model of the resulting stabilizer dislocation suggest only a modest increase in logical error rate (Methods).
We implement the $\sigma$ rotation in a similar manner, using the east and west boundaries instead.
For the larger octagon modules, which would be internally rotated by $\rho$ and $\sigma$, we find that most tilings we consider admit an alternative, smaller group of symmetries that still moves every module onto every other while preserving internal module orientation, typically needing one additional seam on each side of a module (Supplementary Information). This smaller group requires two to four seed cycles rather than one to cover the same logical basis, which can still be measured by a single ribbon extractor by adding additional seams to its boundaries.
Bringing any basis cycle of the $[[3600,104,22]]$ or $[[6400,104,30]]$ code under the ribbon extractor, starting from any other, takes a mean of 4.4 and at most six $\rho$, $\rho^{-1}$, $\sigma$ or $\sigma^{-1}$ moves (\Cref{tab:candidates_888}), with each move transporting four logical operators simultaneously.

Because every logical qubit in this canonical basis has both its $\overline{X}_i$ and $\overline{Z}_i$ cycles in the orbit of the seed cycle, any individual logical qubit can be selectively teleported into and out of the hyperbolic memory by coupling the ribbon extractor to a small compute region, such as a Euclidean surface or color code, enabling universal computation with low space overhead.

As a benchmark, we consider a widely used arithmetic subroutine in quantum algorithms and compile a 50-bit ripple-carry adder~\cite{cuccaro2004new} onto the $[[3600,104,22]]$ ($L=3$) and $[[6400,104,30]]$ ($L=4$) memories derived from the same tiling, each using a single ribbon extractor to teleport operands into and out of a small compute region (Methods).
Since the symmetry group is non-Abelian, the number of moves between two cycles depends on where the extractor is attached (Methods).
By choosing the extractor location so that the two cycles of every symplectic pair are only a single automorphism move apart, and storing operands that are accessed in close succession in the same symplectic pair, we bound the time for the ribbon measurements and automorphisms by $21{,}402$ and $28{,}930$ QEC cycles respectively (428 and 579 per bit).

\section{Discussion and Outlook}

While partitioning quantum processors into manageable modules in solid state systems is known to be important for alleviating constraints on fabrication, yield and coupler density, our architecture demonstrates this modularity can also be exploited to deliver substantial efficiency gains in quantum error correction.
Realizing these savings in a superconducting quantum computer will require a fully integrated system that simultaneously operates high-performance local grids of qubits~\cite{surface_code_below_threshold} and high-fidelity inter-module couplers~\cite{Zhong_2021, Niu_2023, Qiu_2025}.
These systems must also be resilient to fabrication defects, such as broken qubits or couplers.
However, we expect that existing methods for handling fabrication defects in planar color codes~\cite{wolanski2025automated} and surface codes~\cite{auger2017fabrication,strikis2023fabrication,Debroy2025luciinsurfacecode,leroux2024snakes} should be naturally adaptable to modular hyperbolic codes, since the local code structure is similar.

While our implementation of neural network and correlated matching decoders demonstrates that efficient and accurate decoding is possible, real-time decoding at scale will require parallelization or specialized hardware implementations.
Since the full distance of a modular hyperbolic code is distributed across many modules, sub-distance parallelization strategies will likely be necessary, which generally requires parallelizing the decoding algorithm itself~\cite{wu_fusion_blossom,surface_code_below_threshold} rather than relying on parallel windowing~\cite{Skoric2023parallel,tan23scalable}.

Utility-scale quantum computation requires the ability to implement universal logic fault-tolerantly.
We have demonstrated that this is possible by providing the ingredients for a low-overhead quantum memory, using the ribbon extractor for logical measurement along with circuits for implementing automorphisms.
This architecture is universal and a natural fit for serial quantum algorithms and subroutines, such as addition circuits. However, we see several opportunities to improve on the current design. 
First, our ribbon extractor does not fully exploit the non-local connectivity available at module boundaries. This leaves open the possibility of building more space-efficient extractors that use this connectivity \cite{he2025extractorsqldpcarchitecturesefficient}.
Second, it would be desirable to design extractor systems capable of measuring more complex high-weight logical Pauli operators, either in serial or in parallel, in order to implement certain subroutines such as QROM lookup with lower time overhead.
Finally, these logical primitives should be co-designed with compilations of full quantum algorithms, building on our adder estimate (Methods), to provide a complete resource estimation of boundary-connected module architectures.

In summary, boundary-connected modules turn a constraint of scalable hardware into a resource for quantum error correction.
Our architecture builds hyperbolic codes from flat Euclidean modules, requiring connectivity similar to that of current superconducting processors designed for the surface code, with the hyperbolic geometry realized by the inter-module couplers at their boundaries.
Together with the modular syndrome extraction circuits, decoders and logical measurement methods we develop for this setting, this unlocks the efficiency of high-rate QLDPC codes on superconducting devices.
Therefore, boundary-connected modular architectures substantially lower the cost of reaching utility-scale fault-tolerant quantum computation using near-term hardware.

