\section{Hyperbolic Tilings and Symmetry Groups}\label{sec:hyperbolic_tilings_intro}
We detail the construction of hyperbolic and semi-hyperbolic codes derived from closed hyperbolic tilings. Throughout this work, the symmetry groups of these tilings play a central role. Beyond code construction, they guide module partitioning, inter-module connectivity, and the implementation of fault-tolerant logical gates. 

A tiling of the hyperbolic plane $\mathbb{H}^2$ is a covering of the space by closed polygonal faces that intersect only along their edges. Due to the plane's constant negative curvature, the interior angles of a hyperbolic polygon are smaller than those of its Euclidean counterpart, permitting vertex configurations that cannot be realized on flat surfaces. Hyperbolic tilings can be generated from subgroups of the isometry group of $\mathbb{H}^2$. Such a group acts on the plane with a \emph{fundamental domain}, a closed region whose images under the group cover the plane and overlap only along their boundaries. The tiling is the orbit of this domain under the group, with each tile a distinct image of it, so that the tiles are in bijection with the group elements and the combinatorial structure of the tiling is determined by the structure of the group. 

The tilings we consider arise from \emph{Coxeter groups} acting on a space of constant negative curvature. Their generators are order-two reflections across the edges of a \emph{Coxeter polygon}. This polygon serves as a fundamental domain whose interior angles are of the form $\pi/n_i$ for integers $n_i\geq 2$. The geometry completely determines the group's relations, since composing reflections across two mirrors intersecting at angle $\pi/n_i$ yields a rotation of order $n_i$. In this work, we focus on two of the simplest cases of these structures: \emph{triangle groups} and \emph{quadrilateral groups}.

\subsection{Triangle Groups}\label{sec:triangle_groups}

A hyperbolic triangle group, denoted $\triangle(r,s,t)$, is generated by reflections $a,b,c$ in the three sides of a hyperbolic triangle with interior angles $\pi/r, \pi/s, \pi/t$ satisfying $1/r + 1/s + 1/t < 1$. Taking this triangle as the fundamental domain and repeatedly reflecting it across its edges produces an infinite tiling (see \Cref{fig:triangle_group}). In the resulting tiling, $2r$, $2s$ and $2t$ triangles meet at the respective vertices, and each triangle copy corresponds uniquely to a group element of $\triangle(r,s,t)$. The group presentation is given by: 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:triangle_group}
    \triangle(r,s,t) = \left\langle\, a, b, c \;\middle|\;
    a^2 = b^2 = c^2 = (ab)^r = (bc)^s = (ca)^t = e
    \,\right\rangle,
\end{equation}
where $a$, $b$, $c$ are reflections in the three edges of the triangle and $e$ is the identity element. Composing two adjacent reflections yields a rotation about their shared vertex. This defines the corner rotations $\rho = ab$, $\sigma = bc$, $\tau = ca$ by angles $2\pi/r$, $2\pi/s$, $2\pi/t$ respectively. The reflections $a$, $b$, $c$ are orientation-reversing and the rotations $\rho$, $\sigma$, $\tau$ are orientation-preserving, therefore $\triangle(r,s,t)$ contains both orientation-preserving and orientation-reversing isometries.  The orientation-preserving subgroup, denoted $\triangle^+(r,s,t)$, is an index-2 subgroup generated by the corner rotations. Since $\rho\sigma\tau = (ab)(bc)(ca) = e$, the third rotation is redundant, $\tau = (\rho\sigma)^{-1}$, and two generators suffice:
\begin{equation}
    \triangle^+(r,s,t) = \left\langle\, \rho, \sigma \;\middle|\;
    \rho^r = \sigma^s = (\rho\sigma)^t= e
    \,\right\rangle.
\end{equation}
Geometrically, $\triangle^+(r,s,t)$ acts on a fundamental domain consisting of two reflected copies of the original triangle glued along a shared edge, so that each of its elements is a word of even length in $a, b, c$ (see \Cref{fig:orientation_preserving_poincare_cayley_graph_it58}).

\subsubsection{Wythoff Construction}

Uniform tilings of $\mathbb{H}^2$ can be generated from a triangle group by the \emph{Wythoff kaleidoscopic construction}. A uniform tiling can be described by its vertex configuration $p_1.p_2\dots p_k$, which specifies the sequence of $p_i$-sided polygons meeting in cyclic order around every vertex. Alternatively, for regular tilings composed of a single polygon type with $q$ regular $p$-gons meeting at every vertex, we can denote them more compactly by the Schl\"{a}fli symbol $\{p,q\}$. 

The Wythoff construction proceeds by placing a vertex inside the fundamental triangle, called the \emph{generator point}, and drawing a perpendicular line from it to each triangle edge. The orbit of the generator point and its associated perpendicular lines under the group action of $\triangle(r,s,t)$ produces the vertex and edge sets of the tiling $\mathcal{T}=(V,E,F)$. The specific Wythoff tiling produced depends on the placement of the generator point relative to the triangle's edges, as summarized in \Cref{tab:wythoff}. When the point lies in the interior of the fundamental triangle, it rests on no mirrors. Consequently, every corner contributes its full ring of reflected copies, yielding faces with $2r, 2s,$ and $2t$ sides. This generates the omnitruncated tiling with a vertex configuration of $2r.2s.2t$, meaning polygons with $2r, 2s,$ and $2t$ sides meet at every vertex in cyclic order. \Cref{fig:triangle_group} illustrates this interior placement for the $\triangle(4,4,4)$ group, where $r=s=t=4$ yields the regular 8.8.8 (or $\{8,3\}$) tiling in which three octagons meet at every vertex. 

Moving the generator point onto a mirror (triangle edge) or into a corner (triangle vertex) produces the other uniform tilings of the group. When the point lies on a single mirror, it remains fixed under the corresponding reflection. As a result, the polygonal rings around the two corners on that mirror are halved, while the ring at the opposite corner remains full. If the point sits precisely at a corner, it lies on two mirrors simultaneously: the rings at the other two corners are halved, and the occupied corner itself no longer generates a face. These seven possible generator positions and their corresponding tilings are detailed in \Cref{tab:wythoff}. As an illustrative example, \Cref{fig:wythoff_246} depicts the complete Wythoff family for the $\triangle(2,4,6)$ group.

