\subsection{Quadrilateral Groups}\label{sec:quadrilateral_groups}
A quadrilateral group is generated by reflections $a, b, c, d$ across the four sides of a quadrilateral with interior angles $\pi/r$, $\pi/s$, $\pi/t$, $\pi/u$. The group presentation is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:quad_group}
    \square(r,s,t,u) = \left\langle\, a, b, c, d \;\middle|\;
    a^2 = b^2 = c^2 = d^2 = (ab)^r = (bc)^s = (cd)^t = (da)^u = e
    \,\right\rangle,
\end{equation}
where $r, s, t, u$ are the orders of the corner rotations $\rho = ab$, $\sigma = bc$, $\tau = cd$, $\upsilon = da$ respectively, with $\rho\sigma\tau\upsilon = e$. The quadrilateral is hyperbolic when $1/r + 1/s + 1/t + 1/u < 2$. The orientation-preserving subgroup $\square^+(r,s,t,u)$ is an index-2 subgroup, with presentation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:orientable_quad_group}
    \square^+(r,s,t,u) = \left\langle\, \rho, \sigma, \tau \;\middle|\;
    \rho^r = \sigma^s = \tau^t = (\rho\sigma\tau)^u = e
    \,\right\rangle,
\end{equation}
where we have used $\upsilon = (\rho\sigma\tau)^{-1}$. Reflecting the fundamental quadrilateral in its four sides produces a tiling with face configuration $V2r.2s.2t.2u$, where every vertex degree is even (i.e.\ the vertex degrees of a quadrilateral tile are $2r$, $2s$, $2t$, $2u$). Each tile corresponds to a unique group element of $\square(r,s,t,u)$, labeled by a word in the generators $a, b, c, d$ (see \Cref{fig:quadrilateral_group}).

We note that quadrilateral tilings of the hyperbolic plane can also arise from triangle groups, as we discuss in \Cref{sec:quadrilateral_tilings}. The two families of groups are complementary: quadrilateral groups produce tilings whose vertex degrees are always even, including non-regular tilings such as $V4.4.8.8$ from $\square(2,2,4,4)$ (\Cref{fig:quadrilateral_group}), whereas triangle groups can produce only regular or quasi-regular (dual) quadrilateral tilings but can accommodate both even and odd vertex degrees. We also note that a Wythoff construction on the quadrilateral group is also possible, giving the other uniform tilings of the group, but we do not use it in this work.

