\subsection{Dual Tilings}
Every tiling $\mathcal{T} = (V, E, F)$ has a dual tiling $\mathcal{T}^*$, obtained by placing a vertex at the center of each face of $\mathcal{T}$ and connecting two such vertices whenever the corresponding faces share an edge. This exchanges the roles of vertices and faces: the faces of $\mathcal{T}$ become the vertices of $\mathcal{T}^*$, the vertices of $\mathcal{T}$ become the faces of $\mathcal{T}^*$, and each edge of $\mathcal{T}$ is crossed by exactly one dual edge of $\mathcal{T}^*$. Taking the dual twice recovers the original tiling, $(\mathcal{T}^*)^* = \mathcal{T}$. We sometimes use the face-configuration notation to name dual tilings: if $\mathcal{T}$ has vertex configuration $r.s.t$, we write $Vr.s.t$ for its dual $\mathcal{T}^*$. \Cref{fig:octagonal_tiling_and_dual} shows the primal $8.8.8$ (or $\{8,3\}$) tiling, in which three octagons meet at each vertex, together with its dual $V8.8.8$ (or $\{3,8\}$), in which eight triangles meet at each vertex.

