\subsection{Color Code Tilings}\label{sec:color_code_tilings}

To define a valid color code on a two-dimensional tiled surface, the tiling must satisfy two geometric conditions. The tiling must be \emph{trivalent} (three edges meeting at each vertex) and its faces must be 
\emph{three-colorable} such that no two faces that share an edge have the same color \cite{bombin_color}.  We refer to tilings that satisfy these specific geometric properties as \emph{color code tilings}.

To construct hyperbolic color codes, we systematically obtain valid color code tilings for hyperbolic surfaces using the Wythoff construction explained in \Cref{sec:triangle_groups}  \cite{quantum_pin_codes,higgott_2024_floquet}. As shown in \Cref{tab:wythoff}, the interior generator point yields a tiling with the vertex configuration $2r.2s.2t$. Since exactly three faces meet at every vertex, the resulting graph is trivalent. Moreover, the faces of the omnitruncated tiling correspond directly to the three distinct corners of the fundamental triangle. This natural tripartite division guarantees that the faces are three-colorable, making them color code tilings. \Cref{fig:triangle_group} illustrates an example of a color code tiling with octagon faces. The dual of the color tiling is the underlying triangular tiling which we denote $\mathcal{T^\triangle}$. The triangular tiles of $\mathcal{T^\triangle}$ are associated with group elements of $\triangle{(r,s,t)}$. Equivalently, the vertices and edges of the color code tiling $\mathcal{T}$ form the Cayley graph of $\triangle(r,s,t)$: the vertices are the group elements and they are joined by an edge whenever they are related by a generator $a$, $b$ or $c$.

\subsection{Quadrilateral Tilings} \label{sec:quadrilateral_tilings}
A \emph{quadrilateral tiling} $\mathcal{T}^\square$ is a tiling of quadrilateral faces. In this work, we use quadrilateral tilings to construct hyperbolic surface codes. Specifically, we define a hyperbolic surface code on the dual $\mathcal{T} = (\mathcal{T}^\square)^*$ of a quadrilateral tiling $\mathcal{T}^\square$ by associating stabilizer generators with the faces of $\mathcal{T}$. For $\mathcal{T}$ to support a valid surface code, its faces must be \emph{two-colorable}: each face is assigned one of two colors (e.g. red and blue) such that no two faces sharing an edge receive the same color. This ensures that $X$- and $Z$-type stabilizer generators can be assigned to faces of different color, so that stabilizers of opposite type with overlapping support always share an even number of data qubits and therefore commute. The faces of $\mathcal{T}$ are two-colorable whenever the vertices of $\mathcal{T}^\square$ are two-colorable, i.e.\ its edges form a bipartite graph. On the infinite plane this is always the case: every face of $\mathcal{T}^\square$ has even length, so every cycle has even length and the graph is bipartite. On the closed surfaces of \Cref{sec:closed_surfaces} this can fail since the tiling on the surface now has homologically non-trivial cycles, which may sometimes have odd length. Therefore, we check each closed quadrilateral tiling and keep only those that remain bipartite.

Quadrilateral tilings $\mathcal{T}^\square$ of the hyperbolic plane can arise from triangle groups, quadrilateral groups, or in many cases from both, and we construct codes using tilings from both symmetry groups.

The first class of tilings we consider are those that have quadrilateral reflection symmetry. These are generated by a quadrilateral group $\square(r,s,t,u)$ and correspond to tilings where every vertex degree is even, giving the face configuration $V2r.2s.2t.2u$. In this case, a single quadrilateral face is a valid Coxeter polygon and can be taken as the fundamental domain. The tiles are in bijection with the group elements of $\square(r,s,t,u)$, and adjacent tiles are related by right multiplication by a generator (see \Cref{fig:quadrilateral_group}).

