\section{Introduction}
In recent years, understanding the Hamiltonians that model the interactions between particles in emerging quantum devices has become an active research topic. In particular, a rapidly growing literature studies \emph{Hamiltonian learning}, which aims to characterize an unknown Hamiltonian~\cite{Silva2011Practical,holzapfel2015scalable,Zubida2021Optimal,haah2022optimal,yu2023robust,Dutkiewicz.2023,huang2023heisenberg,li2023heisenberglimited,franca2024efficient,Gu2022Practical,zhao2025learning,hu2025ansatz,anshu2021sample,onorati2023efficient,rouze2023learning,bakshi2023learning,ma2024learning,arunachalam2025testing,caro2023learning,sinha2025improvedhamiltonianlearningsparsity,chen2025learning,abbas2025nearly,shin2026heisenberg, shin2026emergent, shin2026hamiltonian, he2026optimal, chen2025lower, lewis2026learning, zhou2026optimal,zhou2026characterizing,romanov2026learning,arad2026near,mobus2026robust}, and \emph{Hamiltonian testing}, which asks whether the Hamiltonian satisfies a given property~\cite{aharonov2022quantum,laborde2022quantum,she2022unitary,bluhm2024hamiltonian,arunachalam2025testing,kallaugher2025hamiltonian,gao2025quantum,sinha2025improvedhamiltonianlearningsparsity,bluhm2026certifying,cai2026optimal,flammia2026autonomous}. Given that the Hamiltonian determines the time evolution of the system, many of those works assume access to the Hamiltonian~$H$ via experiments like those in \cref{fig:access-model}. In those experiments, the algorithm makes several queries to the time evolution operator~$U_H(t),$ only for positive times $t>0$. One of the main focuses of this research line has been to devise protocols with total evolution time scaling like~$1/\eps$ with the precision parameter~$\eps$. Such a scaling is known as the \emph{Heisenberg limit}, and determining in which quantum scenarios it can be attained is also a topic of interest in query complexity~\cite{tang2025amplitude}. So far there are plenty of Hamiltonian learning and testing protocols that achieve the Heisenberg limit, but it is not known whether there is a problem that cannot attain the Heisenberg limit, with the access model of \cref{fig:access-model}. Thus, a natural question is 

\begin{quote}
\centering \emph{Can the Heisenberg limit always be attained in Hamiltonian learning and testing?}
\end{quote}\vspace{0.15cm}

\noindent In this work, we answer this question negatively by showing that Hamiltonian certification and locality testing do not attain the Heisenberg limit. As a byproduct, we also show that quantum amplitude estimation requires inverses, even in the continuous-time query model, recovering a recent result by Tang and Wright \cite{tang2025amplitude}. All three results follow from the hardness of distinguishing the zero Hamiltonian from a suitably chosen ensemble of random Hamiltonians. We establish this hardness by adapting the continuous-time adversary method to forward Hamiltonian evolution, which may be of independent interest and may lead to new lower bounds.

