\title{On The Complexity of Redundancy-Free Quantum Hamiltonians}

\author{Matthew B.~Hastings}
\author{Alexander Schmidhuber}

\begin{abstract}
We consider a class of \emph{redudancy-free} Hamiltonians, 
which are those where the trace vanishes for any product of Hamiltonian terms in which at least one term appears an odd number of times.
This includes Hamiltonians which are sums of products of Paulis with no relations between them.
These Hamiltonians arise naturally in Hamiltonian Decoded Quantum Interferometry \cite{schmidhuber2025}, which separates the hardness of preparing quantum states into two steps: a classical decoding step, and a step preparing the thermofield double state of such a redundancy-free Hamiltonian.

We focus on the
hardness of these Hamiltonians, both for intrinsic interest and to understand the complexity of
the state preparation step in Hamiltonian DQI. We present several results showing that the problem becomes
easy at quadratically lower temperature than for a general Hamiltonian, with the complexity at given inverse temperature
$\beta$ depending on $\beta^2 d$, where $d$ is the degree of the anticommutation graph.  We give an efficient classical algorithm for small $\beta^2 d$ to approximate the partition function, while for large $\beta^2 d$ we show that approximating the partition function is NP-hard. To this end, we introduce what we call an \emph{anticommutation glass}, where frustration arises purely from anticommutation relations. 
We give a subexponential time quantum algorithm to prepare the thermofield state for small $\beta^2 d$, and present some results toward a polynomial time algorithm based on the Feiguin-Klich Hamiltonian. As a result of possible independent interest, we show that this Hamiltonian leads to a polynomial time algorithm for preparing thermofield double states of general Hamiltonians on an interaction graph of degree $d$ for $\beta\lesssim 1/d$. Finally, we show that anticommutation alone suffices to mimic the hardness of general quantum Hamiltonians: estimating the ground state energy of redundancy-free Hamiltonians is QMA-complete.

\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction and Definitions}
We consider a class of Hamiltonians of the form
\be
H=\sum_i J_i O_i,
\label{eq:one}
\ee
where the $J_i$ are scalars and $\Vert O_i \Vert \leq 1$,
such that the trace vanishes for any product of terms in the Hamiltonian in which some term appears an odd number of times.
We call these Hamiltonians \emph{redundancy-free Hamiltonians}\footnote{We choose to call these Hamiltonians redundancy-free instead of relation-free since individual terms might still satisfy certain relations. For instance, Pauli operators always square to the identity. }. 
One important special case is where
the $O_i$ are products of Paulis, but with no relations between the $O_i$, meaning that
no nontrivial product of the $O_i$ is equal to the identity; we call these Pauli redudancy-free.
Another interesting case is to take a Hamitonian which is an arbitrary sum of terms $H=\sum_i J_i O_i$ without imposing any requirement on the traces and define a new Hamiltonian $\sum_i J_i Z_{{\rm anc},i} O_i$, where $Z_{{\rm anc},i}$ is a Pauli $Z$ operator acting on an extra ancilla qubit, one for each term in the Hamiltonian.

A primary motivation for studying redundancy-free Hamiltonians is the quantum algorithm Hamiltonian Decoded Quantum Interferometry (HDQI) introduced in Ref.~\onlinecite{schmidhuber2025}. For a generic Hamiltonian, HDQI prepares its Gibbs state (and other polynomial filters) by first preparing a ``pilot state" that encodes the anticommutation structure of $H$, and subsequently solving a classical decoding problem defined by the the relations between the terms in the Hamiltonian. Understanding the computational complexity of the pilot state preparation was left as a major open question in Ref.~\onlinecite{schmidhuber2025}, and the lack of general techniques for this problem  limited the applicability of HDQI outside of the commuting or nearly-commuting setting. 

We observe that the pilot state in HDQI is equivalent to the thermofield double (a purification of the Gibbs state) of a certain redundancy-free relaxation of the Hamiltonian. HDQI therefore reduces the preparation of the thermofield double of a general Hamiltonian to that of a redundancy-free one by classically decoding its relations. The two steps carry two different 
sources of hardness: anticommutation relations versus classical redundancies between the terms of the Hamiltonian. 

In this paper, we characterize the hardness of the first step. Beyond the algorithmic implications for HDQI, an additional motivation is to understand the physical properties (such as frustration, hysteresis, and entanglement) that arise from anticommutation relations alone, even in the absence of any redundancies.

In the Pauli redundancy-free case,
 a set of operators $O_i$ as in \eqref{eq:one} is characterized by an \emph{anticommutation graph} $G$.  This is a graph with one vertex per operator $O_i$, labeling the vertex by the index $i$, and with an edge between vertices $i,j$ if $\{O_i,O_j\}=0$ and with no edge if instead $[O_i,O_j]=0$.
Given an anti-commutation graph with $n$ vertices, one possible representation of the operators $O_i$ on $n$ qubits is given by taking
\be
\label{Oirep}
O_i=X_i \prod_{j<i, (i,j)\in E} Z_j,
\ee
where $(i,j) \in E$ indicates that the edge from $i$ to $j$ is in the edgeset $E$ of the graph. 
For more general redundancy-free Hamiltonians, we instead have a \emph{non-commutation graph}, so that operators commute unless they are neighbors, in which case no specific commutation relation is imposed. In the Pauli setting, we call \eqref{Oirep} the canonical redundancy-free representation of a graph, and, for a general Pauli Hamiltonian $H$, we call the canonical redundancy-free representation of its anticommutation graph the \textit{redundancy-free relaxation} of $H$.  

For the rest of the paper, we will, for simplicity and for unity of presentation,  consider the Pauli case and refer to anticommutation graphs.  However, all our results about complexity of evaluating the partition function will apply in the more general case. 

For normalization, we fix $|J_i|\leq 1$ for all $i$.  (Indeed, due to the absence of relations, the sign of each $J_i$ is irrelevant for thermal properties of the systems and for any $i$, in the representation above the operator $Z_i$ anticommutes with $O_i$ and commutes with $O_j$ for $j\neq i$).

We are interested in the complexity of computing properties of these Hamiltonans, such as approximating the partition function.  Of course, if all $O_i$ commute, then the problem is trivial, reducing to $n$ decoupled degrees of freedom. Accordingly, the ``pilot state'' of standard DQI~\cite{Jordan2025DQI} is a simple superposition of Dicke states.

We consider the complexity of the problem as a function of the degree $d$ of the anticommutation graph. 
Define
\be
\kappa=\beta^2 d.
\ee
The central theme of our results is that the problem is ``easy" at sufficiently small $\kappa$, i.e., for $\beta$ sufficiently small compared to $1/\sqrt{d}$.
This contrasts with general Hamiltonians, where the problem is only ``easy" for $\beta$ sufficiently small compared to $1/d$.
This gives evidence that Hamiltonian Decoded Quantum Interferometry may simplify the preparation of thermal states of certain Hamiltonians, as this redundancy-free Hamiltonians may be ``easy" at $\beta$ where the decoding step is efficient. 

 In \cref{sec:polymer}, we show that for sufficiently small $\kappa$, a polymer expansion for the free energy converges, giving an efficient classical algorithm to approximate the partition function; the time is polynomial in both $n$ and the error $\epsilon$.
 Conversely, for $\kappa$ sufficiently large, we show in \cref{sec:hardness} that the problem is NP-hard. We start by giving physics arguments and numerical simulations, arguing that we can effectively encode the partition function of an arbitrary Ising Hamiltonian, with both ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic ferms, into one of these redundancy-free Hamiltonians.  This is perhaps slightly surprising: such an Ising Hamiltonian is a sum of terms $Z_i$ or $Z_i Z_j$, with some coefficients, and it \emph{has redundancy} among the terms in the Hamiltonian, but here we encode it into a problem without redundancy. We then make this intuition precise by giving a formal proof of hardness. Moreover, we show that the ground state energy estimation problem of redundancy-free Hamiltonians remains QMA-hard.

In \cref{sec:QSP}, we consider the problem of preparing a thermofield double state on a quantum computer, and give a subexponential algorithm to do this for sufficiently small $\kappa$.  In \cref{sec:beyond}, we present some results towards improving the runtime of the algorithm to polynomial.  While we do not succeed in doing this for redundancy-free Hamiltonians at $\beta \lesssim 1/\sqrt{d}$, we do
give an
algorithm for efficient preparation of thermofield double states of general Hamiltonians on an interaction graph of degree $d$ for $\beta \lesssim 1/d$.  This algorithm can be compared to
Ref.~\onlinecite{rouze2025efficient} which also works for $\beta \lesssim 1/d$; however, our algorithm may have better scaling for high-dimensional lattices as the allowed $\beta$ in Ref.~\onlinecite{rouze2025efficient} depends exponentially on lattice dimension while we make no assumption on lattice dimension.
See also Ref.~\onlinecite{bakshi2024high}.

Note on use of AI: most of the quantum Monte Carlo code was written by AI, and AI was used to find some references on the polymer expansion. AI was also used to help with language editing and proofreading in certain sections. No other use was made of AI in this project.

\section{Hamiltonian Decoded Quantum Interferometry}
\label{sec:hdqi_background}
In this section, we briefly explain how redundancy-free Hamiltonians arise in Hamiltonian Decoded Quantum Interferometry.
Decoded quantum interferometry (DQI) prepares a state whose amplitudes are a polynomial of a classical cost function by coherently running a classical decoder~\cite{Jordan2025DQI}. Hamiltonian DQI (HDQI) extends this idea to polynomials of quantum Hamiltonians~\cite{schmidhuber2025}. The quantum input to this procedure is the so-called pilot state, which captures the anticommutation structure of the Hamiltonian and for thermal states is Clifford-equivalent to the thermofield double (TFD) of its redundancy-free relaxation. The decoder then removes the extra register that distinguishes products related in the physical Hamiltonian. For more details on HDQI, consult Ref.~\onlinecite{schmidhuber2025}. For simplicity, we focus on the Pauli setting throughout this section. 

Write the Hamiltonian on $n$ qubits as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_hamiltonian}
H_v=\sum_{i=1}^m J_i v_iP_i,
\qquad J_i\geq0,\qquad v_i\in\{\pm1\},
\end{equation}
where the $P_i$ are Hermitian Pauli operators. Let $b_i=(x_i,z_i)\in\mathbb F_2^{2n}$ record the $X$ and $Z$ exponents of $P_i$, ignoring its scalar phase. Multiplication adds these labels. Set $B=(b_1\ \cdots\ b_m)$. The \emph{relation code} is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_relation_code}
\mathcal C=\ker B.
\end{equation}
Indeed, the ordered product $P(y)=P_1^{y_1}\cdots P_m^{y_m}$ is scalar exactly when $By=0$. Decoding means recovering $y$ from its syndrome $By$.

Let $A$ be the anticommutation matrix, with $P_iP_j=(-1)^{A_{ij}}P_jP_i$. On $m$ qubits take the canonical redundancy-free generators and Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_relaxation}
\widehat P_i=X_i\prod_{j<i}Z_j^{A_{ij}},
\qquad
\widehat H=\sum_iJ_i\widehat P_i.
\end{equation}
Their ordered products $\widehat P(y)=\widehat P_1^{y_1}\cdots\widehat P_m^{y_m}$ are orthonormal for normalized trace. Expand
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_thermal_expansion}
e^{-\beta\widehat H/2}=\sum_y a_y\widehat P(y),
\qquad
\widehat Z=\tr(e^{-\beta\widehat H})=\sum_y|a_y|^2.
\end{equation}
Reordering a word uses only the pairwise commutation signs, which are the same for the physical and relaxed generators. The same coefficients therefore give
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_physical_expansion}
e^{-\beta H_v/2}=\sum_y a_yv^yP(y),
\qquad v^y=\prod_i v_i^{y_i}.
\end{equation}
The expansion is worked out in Appendix~\ref{app:hdqi_expansion}.

For an operator $Q$ on $k$ qubits, write $|Q\rangle=(Q\otimes I)\Psi_{\rm ME}$, where $\Psi_{\rm ME}=2^{-k/2}\sum_z|z\rangle_L|z\rangle_R$. Then $\langle Q|R\rangle=\tr(Q^\dagger R)$. The redundancy-free TFD is $|\widehat\psi_\beta\rangle=|e^{-\beta\widehat H/2}\rangle/\sqrt{\widehat Z}$, while the HDQI pilot and its error distribution are
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_pilot}
|\pi_\beta\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt{\widehat Z}}\sum_y a_y|y\rangle,
\qquad
\mu_\beta(y)=\frac{|a_y|^2}{\widehat Z}.
\end{equation}
A Clifford circuit converts one state into the other. To see this, write
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_vectorized_product}
|\widehat P(y)\rangle
=2^{-m/2}\sum_z
(-1)^{\sum_{j<i}A_{ij}y_i z_j}
|y+z\rangle_L|z\rangle_R.
\end{equation}
CNOTs from $R_i$ to $L_i$ put $y$ in the left register. A controlled-$Z$ between $L_i$ and $R_j$ for each $j<i$ with $A_{ij}=1$ removes the phase, and Hadamards on the right register give $|y\rangle|0^m\rangle$. Calling this circuit $W_A$, we have
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_clifford}
W_A|\widehat\psi_\beta\rangle
=|\pi_\beta\rangle|0^m\rangle.
\end{equation}
The same equivalence holds for any redundancy-free Pauli realization of the generators, as shown in Appendix~\ref{app:hdqi_expansion}.

The remaining algorithimic steps of HDQI are as follows. Starting with the pilot, apply the phase $v^y$ and the controlled ordered product $P(y)$ to one half of $n$ Bell pairs. A Bell basis change exposes the label $By$. We compute $\operatorname{Dec}(By)$, add it to the pilot register, and undo the decoder workspace and the Bell basis change. This gives
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_coherent_output}
|\Psi_v\rangle
=\frac1{\sqrt{\widehat Z}}\sum_y a_yv^y
|y+\operatorname{Dec}(By)\rangle\,|P(y)\rangle.
\end{equation}
Correctly decoded labels all leave the first register in $|0^m\rangle$, so their contributions interfere. We discard the first register at the end. After preparing the redundancy-free TFD, the cost is polynomial in $m+n$ plus the cost of running the decoder reversibly.

When the terms commute, $\widehat H=\sum_iJ_iX_i$. The pilot is a product state, with its $i$th factor proportional to $\cosh(\beta J_i/2)|0\rangle-\sinh(\beta J_i/2)|1\rangle$. Its bits are independent and have error probabilities
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_bsc}
p_i=\frac{\sinh^2(\beta J_i/2)}{\cosh(\beta J_i)}
=\frac{1-\operatorname{sech}(\beta J_i)}2
=O(\beta^2 J_i^2).
\end{equation}
For equal coupling magnitudes this is a binary symmetric channel. Hence HDQI applied to commuting Hamiltonian gives a  conceptually simple picture: the pilot state is always efficiently preparable since there are no anti-commutation relations, and the decoding problem to reach inverse temperature $\beta$ is that of the binary symmetric channel (BSC). As $\beta \to \infty$ so physical temperature approaches 0, the BSC noise rate approaches the information-theoretic limit $p_i = 1/2$.

Noncommuting terms introduce correlations such that the pilot and error distribution  do not have this simple product structure, but (as shown in Appendix~\ref{app:hdqi_weights}) the error distribution $\mu_\beta$ still satisfies
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_local_stochastic}
\Pr_{Y\sim\mu_\beta}[S\subseteq\operatorname{supp}Y]
\leq\prod_{i\in S}\left(\frac{\beta J_i}{2}\right)^2
\qquad(S\subseteq[m]).
\end{equation}
For small $\beta$, both these results achieve quadratically better scaling in $\beta$ than the generic scheme first introduced in Hamiltonian DQI, which approximates a Gibbs state by choosing a polynomial approximation to the exponential function instead of working with the exact expansion \eqref{eq:hdqi_physical_expansion}.

 We focus on Gibbs states and exponential functions in this document for physical and conceptual simplicity; every result here directly extends to arbitrary polynomials as studied in HDQI. For a degree-$\ell$ polynomial, the pilot has a sharp weight cutoff: $a_y=0$ for $|y|>\ell$. The thermal pilot instead has a small tail at high weights. To show that this does not impact the applicability of HDQI, at least in the setting of randomly signed couplings, we prove in Appendix~\ref{app:hdqi_robustness} the following robustness theorem. In words, for a Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
H_v=\sum_{i=1}^m v_iJ_iP_i
\end{equation}
with uniformly random signs $v$, HDQI prepares the thermofield double of $H_v$ to high fidelity for all but a $\delta$-fraction of sign choices, provided (1) the  thermofield double of the redundancy-free relaxation is prepared with high fidelity and (2) the decoder succeeds with high probability. The two sources of error simply add: imperfections in the pilot are not amplified by decoding failures, and the decoder only needs to succeed whp on average, not in the worst case.

\begin{theorem}[Robustness of HDQI]
\label{thm:hdqi_robustness}
Fix $\beta\geq0$, the operators $P_i$, the magnitudes $J_i$, and a decoder $\operatorname{Dec}$, and let
\[
r=\Pr_{Y\sim\mu_\beta}[\operatorname{Dec}(BY)\neq Y].
\]
Choose the signs $v_i$ independently and uniformly. With the exact pilot, the physical output $\rho_v$ of \eqref{eq:hdqi_coherent_output} satisfies
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_average_infidelity}
\mathbb E_v\bigl[1-\langle\psi_{\beta,v}|\rho_v|\psi_{\beta,v}\rangle\bigr]
\leq2r,
\qquad
|\psi_{\beta,v}\rangle=
\frac{|e^{-\beta H_v/2}\rangle}{\sqrt{\tr(e^{-\beta H_v})}}.
\end{equation}
If the prepared redundancy-free TFD has squared fidelity at least $1-\varepsilon_{\rm p}$, the resulting output $\widetilde\rho_v$ obeys
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hdqi_robustness}
D_{\rm tr}\!\left(\widetilde\rho_v,
|\psi_{\beta,v}\rangle\langle\psi_{\beta,v}|\right)
\leq\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\rm p}}+\sqrt{\frac{2r}{\delta}}
\end{equation}
with probability at least $1-\delta$ over the signs, for every $0<\delta<1$. Here $D_{\rm tr}(\rho,\sigma)=\tfrac12\operatorname{Tr}|\rho-\sigma|$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Polymer Expansion at Small $\kappa$}
\label{sec:polymer}
The partition function $Z=\tr(\exp[\beta H])$ can be expanded as a power series
$$
Z=1+\sum_{k\geq 1} \frac{\beta^k}{k!} \tr(H^k).
$$
In turn, $\tr(H^k)$ can be written as a sum of $n^k$ terms,
$$\tr(H^k)=\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_k} \prod_{a=1}^k J_{i_a} \tr(O_{i_1} O_{i_2} \ldots O_{i_k}).$$
The trace vanishes unless each operator $O_i$ appears an even number of times, in which case the trace is equal to $\pm 1$ for the Pauli case and is bounded in absolute value by $1$ in general.
Any given choice of $i_1,\ldots,i_k$ induces some subgraph $G_{i_1,\ldots,i_k}$ of the anticommutation graph $G$.  This subgraph has a vertex $i$ iff $i\in \{i_1,\ldots,i_k\}$, and has an edge $(i,j)$ between two such vertices iff $\{O_i,O_j\}=0$.

Write the vertex set $V_{i_1,\ldots,i_k}$ of this subgraph as a union of connected components; here connectivity is with respect to this subgraph $G_{i_1,\ldots,i_k}$.

\subsection{The Polymer Partition Function}
We now introduce the polymer partition function.

Consider any given sequence $i_1,\ldots,i_k$ where the vertex set has $C$ connected components.  Consider any other
sequence $j_1,\ldots,j_k$ that can be obtained from $i_1,\ldots,i_k$ by a permutation that \emph{preserves the order in each connected component}.
For example, if $C=2$ and $i=1,i=2$ are in one component and $i=3,i=4$ are in another component, then $1,2,1,2,3,4,3,4$ can be permuted to $1,3,2,4,1,3,2,4$ as the order $1,2,1,2$ and $3,4,3,4$ is the same in both.
Then, both sequences give the same term:  $\beta^k \prod_{a=1}^k J_{i_a} \tr(O_{i_1} O_{i_2} \ldots O_{i_k})=\beta^k \prod_{a=1}^k J_{j_a} \tr(O_{j_1} O_{j_2} \ldots O_{j_k})$.
Let's count the number of sequences that can be obtained from a given $i_1,\ldots,i_k$ by such a permutation.
Suppose the sequence $i_1,\ldots,i_k$ has $n_c$ entries in the $c$-th connected component, counting multiplicity.  For example, the sequence $1,2,1,2,3,4,3,4$ has $n_1=n_2=4$.  Given $i_1,\ldots,i_k$, we can specify any sequence $j_1,\ldots,j_k$ simply by indicating which component each term of $j_1,\ldots,j_k$ is from.  That is, by giving a sequence of $k$ numbers, each from $1,\ldots,C$, with $n_1$ appearances of $1$, $n_2$ appearances of $2$, and so on.
The number of such sequences is equal to $k!/(\prod_{c=1}^C n_c!)$.

Thus, for any given sequence $i_{1,1},\ldots,i_{1,n_1},i_{2,1},\ldots,i_{2,n_2},\ldots,i_{C,1},\ldots,i_{C,n_C}$, written so that $i_{a,1},\ldots,i_{a,n_a}$ are all in the $a$-th connected component, if we sum over all sequences related by such a permutation to that given sequence,
the total contribution to $Z$ is equal to
$$\prod_{c=1}^C \Bigl( \frac{1}{n_c!} \prod_{a=1}^{n_c} (\beta J_{i_{c,a}}) \tr(O_{i_{c,1}} O_{i_{c,2}} \ldots O_{i_{c,k}})\Bigr).$$

We can then express the sum over all contributions to $Z$ by summing over the number of connected components, summing over choices of connected components, summing over sequences in each connected component, and for each choice of a sequence in each connected component, using the above expression for the contribution to $Z$.
We can replace the sum over sequences in each connected component by a sum over multisets (i.e., sets of vertices with multiplicities), followed by a sum over sequences in which each element of that multiset appears a number of times equal to its multiplicity.  We say that such sequences are \emph{compatible} with the given multiset.
Formally, let $\Gamma$ be the set of all finite multisets $\gamma$ such that the subgraph induced by $\gamma$ (i.e., the subgraph containing the vertices which appear at least once in that multiset)  has exactly one connected component.
Let the weight $w(\gamma)$, where $\gamma$ is a given multiset, be given by
$$w(\gamma)=\frac{1}{m!} \; \sum_{\stackrel{i_1,\ldots,i_m}{ {\rm compatible\, with\,} \gamma}}\;  \prod_{a=1}^{m} (\beta J_{i_a}) \tr(O_{i_{1}} \ldots O_{i_m}),$$
where $m$ is the total multiplicity of the multiset
Note that we can bound the weight $w(\gamma)$ by the product $\prod_i (\beta J_{i})^{m_i}$, where $m_i$ is the multiplicity of $i$ in the given multiset, since there are at most $m!$ different sequences compatible with the given multiset.
Since $|J_i|\leq 1$, this bounds $w(\gamma) \leq \beta^{\sum_i m_i}$.

Then,
\be
\label{Zpolymer}
Z=\sum_{\Gamma'\subset \Gamma} \bigl( \prod_{\gamma\in \Gamma'} w(\gamma)\bigr) \bigl(\prod_{\{\gamma,\gamma'\}\subset \Gamma'}\delta(\gamma,\gamma'),
\ee
where here $\delta(\gamma,\gamma')$ enforces the requirement that different components must be non-adjacent to each other.  We set $\delta(\gamma,\gamma')=0$ if some vertex in $\gamma$ is equal to or adjacent to some vertex in $\gamma'$.
Otherwise we set it equal to $1$.
We include the term $\Gamma'=\emptyset$ in this sum, giving a contribution of $1$ to the partition function.

\Cref{Zpolymer} is in the form of a polymer partition function.  Each $\gamma$ is called a \emph{polymer}.
A theory has been developed in statistical mechanics studying these partition functions.  See for example chapter 5 in Ref.~\onlinecite{friedli_velenik_2017} for a review.

Before proceeding and using this theory,
it is convenient to further group all multisets having the same support into one polymer.  From here on, $\gamma$ will label then a set of sites, rather than a multiset.  Summing over multisets having the given support, summing $m_i=2,4,\ldots$ for each $i$,
we bound
\be
w(\gamma) \geq \bigl(\frac{ \beta^2}{1-\beta^2}\bigr)^{|\gamma|},
\ee
where $|\gamma|$ is the cardinality of the support.  Note here that we use that all $m_i$ must be even or else the trace $\tr(O_{i_{1}} \ldots O_{i_m})$.  \emph{This requirement that each $m_i$ be even is the key idea that leads to convergence at $\beta\sim 1/\sqrt{d}$}.

The key idea in this theory is roughly as follows.  If the weights are small, one might hope to develop a good approximation theory, using a small number of terms in the sum, i.e., sets $\Gamma'$ which have small cardinality.  However, if we directly study the partition function, the small weights are counteracted by the possible large number of polymers.  Just to give an example, suppose we take all operators $O_i$ to commute, i.e., $O_i=Z_i$, and let all $J=1$.  Then, the simplest polymer repeats some given index twice, and has weight $\beta^2$.  For small $\beta$, this weight is small, but the sum over all terms with $|\Gamma'|=1$ is equal to $n\beta^2$ and hence is not small for large enough $n$, and then the next term with two polymers is $\approx n^2 \beta^4/2$ and may be even larger.
However, if we study the logarithm of the partition function, these problems are resolved in many cases, and the series converges rapidly if the weights are small enough and if each polymer interacts with few enough other polymers.  Here the interaction between polymers is due to the requirement that the different components be non-adjacent to each other, i.e., it is caused by non-zero $\zeta(\gamma,\gamma')$, where
$\zeta(\gamma,\gamma')\equiv 1-\delta(\gamma,\gamma')$.

\subsection{The Series for the Log Partition Function and a Convergence Criterion}
It is proven that, under a certain convergence condition $(*)$ given below,
we can write $Z=\exp[F]$, where $F$ has the power series expansion
\be
\label{Fexpansion}
F=\sum_{m\geq 1} \sum_{\gamma_1,\ldots \gamma_m} \phi(\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_m) \prod_i w(\gamma_i),
\ee
where $\phi(\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_m)$ is defined as follows.  If $m=1$, $\phi(\gamma)=1$.  If $m\geq 1$, define $\phi(\cdot)$ as follows.  Let $\sum_{G_{{\rm conn},m}}$ denote the sum over graphs $G_{{\rm conn},m}$ which are connected and which have $m$ vertices, labeled $1,\ldots,m$.  Then for $m\geq 2$,
$$\phi(\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_m)=\frac{1}{m!} \sum_{G_{{\rm conn},m}} \prod_{(i,j)\in G_{{\rm conn},m}} \zeta(\gamma_i,\gamma_j).$$

The required convergence condition $(*)$ is that there exist some positive real number $a(\gamma)$ for each $\gamma$ so
that
\be
\label{convcon}
\sum_{\gamma' \neq \gamma} |w(\gamma')| e^{a(\gamma')} \zeta(\gamma,\gamma')\leq a(\gamma).
\ee
Under this assumption, it is proven (see theorem 5.4 in \cite{friedli_velenik_2017}) that the expansion \cref{Fexpansion} converges absolutely, and that $Z=\exp[F]$.
Further, it is proven that 
\be
1+\sum_{k\geq 2} k \sum_{\gamma_2,\ldots,\gamma_k} |\phi(\gamma_1,\gamma_2,\ldots,\gamma_k)| \prod_{j=2}^k |w(\gamma_j)| \leq e^{a(\gamma_1)}
\ee
for all $\gamma_1$, and hence
\be
\label{oneterm}
w(\gamma_1)+\sum_{k\geq 2} k \sum_{\gamma_2,\ldots,\gamma_k} |\phi(\gamma_1,\gamma_2,\ldots,\gamma_k)| \prod_{j=1}^k |w(\gamma_j)| \leq e^{a(\gamma_1)} w(\gamma_1)
\ee
\cref{oneterm} bounds the sum of absolute values of terms in \cref{Fexpansion} which include polymer $\gamma_1$.

Indeed, \cref{convcon} does hold for sufficiently small $\kappa$ if we pick
\be
a(\gamma)=|\gamma|.
\ee
To show this,
we need a bound on the number of connected subsets of the anticommutation graph.  
Such subsets are called lattice animals.  Using standard bounds, the number of polymers $\gamma$ with cardinality $|\gamma|=W$ which include some given 
vertex of the anticommutation graph
is bounded by
$C (e d)^{W-1}$ for some constant $C$.
Any polymer $\gamma'$ which has non-vanishing $\zeta(\gamma,\gamma')$ must include some vertex in $\gamma$ or which neighbors $\gamma$.  There are at most $(d+1) |\gamma|$ such vertices.
Hence, the number of polymers $\gamma'$ with non-vanishing $\zeta(\gamma,\gamma')$ and
with given $|\gamma'|=W$ is bounded by $C (d+1) (e d)^{W-1} |\gamma|
\leq C' (e d)^{W}$ for some other constant $C'$,  while the weight of such polymers is bounded by $(\beta/(1-\beta^2))^W$, and of course
$\exp(a(\gamma'))=\exp(W)$.
So, the sum over $\gamma'$ in \cref{convcon} is
bounded by
$$\sum_{W\geq 1}C' \bigl(\frac{e^2d \beta^2}{1-\beta^2}\bigr)^{W}
 |\gamma|,
 $$
 and for
 sufficiently small $\kappa$ this inequality holds.
 Here we are assuming that $d\geq 1$ so that small $\kappa$ also implies $\beta^2<1$.

 Let us know see how rapidly \cref{Fexpansion} converges.
 Consider the sum of all terms in \cref{Fexpansion} which include some given polymer $\gamma$ and where the total cardinality of the polymers appearing in that is equal to some $\Wtot$.
Let $\kappa_0$ be such that the inequality holds for all $\kappa<\kappa_0$.
Then, for any given $\kappa<\kappa_0$, since the sum of these terms converges absolutely for $\kappa<\kappa_0$, indeed the sum is bounded by
$a(\gamma) w(\gamma) (\kappa/\kappa_0)^{\Wtot}$.

Hence, for any fixed ratio $\kappa/\kappa_0<1$, this sum is exponentially small in $\Wtot$, and so we may truncate up to terms of total cardinality of support $<\Wtot$ with exponentially small error.

In particular, the derivative of $F$ with respect to any coupling constant can be expressed as a sum of terms including polymers which include the given vertex, and so if we truncate the series for this at total cardinality of support $<\Wtot$ the error $\epsilon$ is bounded by
$C (\kappa/\kappa_0)^{\Wtot}$ for some constant $C$.
Similarly, the error $\epsilon$ in $F$ if we truncate the series at total cardinality of support $<\Wtot$ is bounded by
$C n (\kappa/\kappa_0)^{\Wtot}$.

\subsection{Complexity of Calculating The Truncation}
\label{compcalc}
We now consider the complexity of computing this truncated series, in particular the sum of all terms appearing in $F$ up to $\Wtot$.
First consider the complexity of computing the weight $w(\gamma)$ of some $\gamma$ with $|\gamma|=W$.
Recall that $w(\gamma)$
equals the sum $\beta^k \prod_{a=1}^k J_{i_a} \tr(O_{i_1} O_{i_2} \ldots O_{i_k})$
over \emph{all} sequences $i_1,\ldots,i_k$ such that each vertex in $\gamma$ appears at least once in the sequence and no vertices outside $\gamma$ appear in the sequence.  We could approximate $w(\gamma)$ by explicitly summing over such sequences, but the number of sequences grows factorially in $|\gamma|$.

A more efficient technique is as follows.   Identify the label $\gamma$ of a polymer with sets of vertices.  For any such set $\gamma$, let $$Z(\gamma,\beta)=\tr(\exp[\beta H(\gamma)]),$$ where $H(\gamma)=\sum_{i\in \gamma} J_i O_i$.
For any $\gamma$, in the Pauli case we can represent the operators $O_i$ for $i\in \gamma$ using $|\gamma|$ qubits and obtain the correct commutation/anticommutation relations; in the general case we must assume some representation of the operators on a bounded number of qudits exists.  Using this representation, we can compute $Z(\gamma,\beta)$ in time $\exp(O(|\gamma|))$ and up to an accuracy $\epsilon$, with the time scaling polylogarithmically in the error.

Of course, the series expansion of $Z(\gamma,\beta)$ includes terms where not every $i\in \gamma$ appears, meaning that the corresponding operator $O_i$ is not in the trace in the given term.
However, we can solve this using an inclusion-exclusion method.
We have
\be
w(\gamma)=\sum_{\gamma'\subset \gamma} (-1)^{|\gamma|-|\gamma'|} Z(\gamma',\beta).
\ee
Since there are only $2^{|\gamma|}$ subsets $\gamma'$ of $\gamma$, the total time to compute $w(\gamma)$ is 
$\exp(O(|\gamma|))$, again up to a polylogarithm in the error.

We now count the number of terms that appear in this truncated series for $F$.  Any term that appears involves some number of polymers, $\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_m$.
These polymers must be connected with each other in the following sense: there is some permutation $\pi$ so that $\gamma_{\pi(i)}$ neighbors $\gamma_{\pi(i+1)}$ for $i=1,\ldots,m-1$, where we say that two polymers neighbor each other if they intersect on a vertex or if some vertex in one neighbors some vertex in another.
Thus, these collection of polymers can be regarded as a lattice animal on an enlarged graph which consists of $m$ copies of the anticommutation graph where we additional add edges between each vertex $v$ in the $i$-th copy with the same vertex $v$ in the $(i+1)$-st copy as well as to all neighbors of $v$ in the $(i+1)$-st copy.  The vertices in the lattice on the enlarged graph in the $i$-th copy are simply those in $\gamma_{\pi(i)}$.
The degree of this enlarged graph is bounded by $3d+2$.
So, we can bound the number of such animals by
$n C (C' d)^W$
for some constants $C,C'$.

Truncating at $W<\Wtot$, and using the exponential complexity of computing each $w(\gamma)$, the complexity of evaluation is bounded by
$$O(n (C d )^{\Wtot})=O(n \exp(O(\log(d)) \Wtot)),$$ for some other constant $C$.

To obtain error $\epsilon$ in $F$ we need
$\Wtot\sim \log(n/\epsilon)/\log(\kappa^{-1}).$
Hence, the complexity is
$$O(n (n/\epsilon)^{O(\log(d)/\log(\kappa^{-1}))}).$$

For any fixed $d,\kappa$, this time is polynomial.

\section{NP-hardness at Large $\kappa$}
\label{sec:hardness}

We now show that approximating the partition function for sufficiently large $\kappa$ is NP-hard.  

We first construct a simple system that in some sense acts as a classical Ising spin: the thermal state of this Hamiltonian has two distinct ``basins".  In a particular representation of the operators $O_i$ that we will use (different from the representation in \cref{Oirep}), these basins will be either $X$ or $Z$ polarized for the spins.  These basins act as the state of this classical Ising spin.  By adjusting the strength of certain coupling terms in the Hamiltonian, we can make one or the other of the basins preferred, acting a magnetic field on this classical Ising spin. For the formal hardness proof, however, it suffices to use equal strengths and no magnetic fields.  Finally, we then show that by adding appropriate coupling terms between a pair of these systems we can effectively induce ferromagnetic or anti-ferromagnetic interactions between these effective Ising spins.
We can then build a system that mimics a classical Ising model by taking many such systems, adding interactions and magnetic fields in this way, and then approximating the partition function is NP-hard; the product of $\beta$ times the effective interaction strength of this effective classical Ising model will be large, growing with $d$ so that model can be in the low temperature regime.
The important point in this construction is that \emph {the effective Ising model can have redundancies}.

The simple system we construct may have independent interest as a quantum spin glass.  It shares some relation to the quantum random energy model\cite{goldschmidt1990solvable}.
The anticommutation graph will have two anticliques, i.e., two sets of operators, where operators in a given set commute with all others in that set, while operators are roughly equally likely to commute or anticommute if they are in different sets.
Frustration and glassiness, including properties such as hysteresis, arises from the anticommutation relation.
Hence, we call it the \emph{anticommutation glass}.
Our construction of the anticommutation glass uses randomness.  However, the randomness is simply a way to obtain some simple system with some useful properties. 
In \cref{sec:NP_formal}, we use an explicit deterministic family of invertible matrices with the same two-family structure to obtain a formal NP-hardness proof. Rather than assuming a decomposition of the physical thermal state into two basins, we will make this picture precise through upper and lower bounds on the full partition function.

The reader interested primarily in the formal hardness proof should read \cref{subsec:ag,subsec:cag} for the physical motivation before proceeding to \cref{sec:NP_formal}, but may skip \cref{subsec:se,subsec:ns,subsec:tm}.
\subsection{Anticommutation Glass}
\label{subsec:ag}
The anticommutation glass Hamiltonian is as follows.  We slightly change normalization and notation in this section.  We have $n$ qubits and $2n$ operators $O_i$, $i=1,\ldots,2n$, for some integer $n$.  
The degree of the anticommutation graph for this Hamiltonian will be bounded by $n$.
When we couple several such anticommutation glasses to produce an effective classical Ising Hamiltonian, the degree of the anticommutation graph will be proportional to $n$ times the degree of the interaction graph of that Ising Hamiltonian.

The first $n$ of these operators are Pauli $X$ operators on a single qubit:
$$1\leq i \leq n  \quad \rightarrow \quad O_i=X_i.$$
On the other hand, for the remaining operators we set
$$
O_{n+i}=D_i\equiv\prod_{j=1}^n Z_j^{A_{ij}},
\qquad 1\leq i\leq n,
$$
where $A$ is an $n$-by-$n$ invertible matrix over $\mathbb F_2$.
The Hamiltonian is
\be
H_A(J)=-\sum_{i=1}^n X_i-J\sum_{i=1}^n D_i,
\ee
with $J>0$.
That is, each operator $O_i$ for $i>n$ is a product of $Z_j$ over $j$ such that $A_{i-n,j}=1$.
In a change of normalization from previously, we will allow $J>1$ so long as it is still bounded by a constant; one can overall rescale $H$ and $\beta$ by this constant to return to the previous normalization.

We  refer to operators $O_i$ with $i\leq n$ as $X$-type operators, while those with $i>n$ are $Z$-type operators.
For $i>n$ and $j\leq n$, the operators $O_i,O_j$ anticommute whenever $A_{i-n,j}=1$.

We choose the matrix $A$ randomly, but require that it be invertible.  In the numerical simulations reported below, we effectively chose such an $A$ by starting with the identity matrix and then doing a very large number (large compared to $n$) of random row operations on the matrix.

The requirement that $A$ be invertible is equivalent to the requirement that there be no relations among the $O_i$.
Choosing $A$ in this random way means that for $i\leq n$ and $j>n$, the pair of operators $O_i$ and $O_j$ are roughly equally likely to commute or anticommute with each other.

Since the matrix $A$ is invertible, we could apply a sequence of CNOT gates to the qubits so that, conjugating by these gates, $O_i=Z_{i-n}$ for $n+1\leq i \leq 2n$.  However, after applying this sequence of gates, we have $O_i = \prod_{j} X_j^{A_{ji}} $ for $1\leq i \leq n$.
We could then follow with a Hadamard on all qubits, turning Pauli $X$ to Pauli $Z$.
Thus, statistically, there is no distinction between $X$-type and $Z$-type operators, other than of course the coupling constant $J$; by applying such a sequence of CNOTs followed by Hadamards, we can interchange $X$-type and $Z$-type operators up to replacing $A$ by its transpose which is statistically the same.
The symmetry between $X$-type and $Z$-type operators is an important difference between this and the quantum random energy model.

We claim that the physical properties of the system depend on $\kappa$ as follows, with these properties observed for almost all random instances.
Consider the expectation value of a given operator, either $X$-type or $Z$-type operator.  If we \emph{only had operators of the given type and not of the other type}, then the expectation value can be determined easily: since the Hamiltonian is redundancy-free, the expectation value would be
$\tanh(\beta)$ for an $X$-type operator and $\tanh(\beta J)$ for a $Z$-type operator.
Let the \emph{ratio} be defined to be the ratio between the observed expectation value and this quantity (either $\tanh(\beta)$ or $\tanh(\beta J)$).
The ratio clearly approaches $1$ as $\kappa\rightarrow 0$; we show below that for small $\kappa$, the ratio is decreasing with $\kappa$ for both types of operators.
However, for $J>1$, for $Z$-type operators, the ratio hits a minimum at some $\kappa$ and then increases, converging to $1$ at large $\kappa$.
At the same time, for $J>1$, for $X$-type operators, the ratio is decreasing for all $\kappa$, converging to $0$ at large $\kappa$.
Note, however, that the individual expectation values of $Z$-type operators are still small compared to $1$ at large $\kappa$, so long as $\beta \ll 1$.
So, it is not that the system is fully polarized in the $Z$-direction, but rather that the $X$-type operators become negligible for the thermal properties, and we have thermal fluctuations in the $Z$-basis about this polarized state.
Conversely, for $J<1$, the same phenomenon is observed with $X$- and $Z$-type interchanged: the ratio for the $Z$-type operators converges to $0$ at large $\kappa$ while that for $X$-type converges to $1$.

Further, we observe numerically some hysteresis.  That is, starting the path-integral Monte Carlo simulations below in a state polarized in the $Z$-direction but with $J<1$ means that it sometimes gets stuck, leaving the ratio for $Z$-type operators close to $1$ at large $\kappa$ and the ratio for $X$-type operators close to $0$, even though in equilibrium the ratios should display the opposite behavior.

For this reason, we expect that, for  large $\kappa$, $J$ close to $1$, and $n$ large, there will be two different states relevant for the thermodynamics, one state stable and one state only metastable.  Call these states $X$-type or $Z$-type, where the $X$-type state is
approximately described by ignoring the $Z$-type operators and considering the thermal fluctuations for the $X$-type operators, or vice-versa.
Indeed, representing the $X$-type operators as $X_i$, the $X$-type state is simply a product state of qubits in an $X$ magnetic field at finite $\beta$. 

Thus, the $X$-type and $Z$-type states of the anticommutation glass act as the two different states of the classical Ising spin system we will build, and $J-1$ acts as an effective magnetic field on this effective classical Ising spin, choosing one or the other state.

\subsection{Coupling Anticommutation Glasses and Effective Classical Ising Model}
\label{subsec:cag}
Now consider two such anticommutation glasses.  Let us show how to couple the effective Ising spins.  We represent the first using unprimed Pauli operators such as $X_i$ and the second using primed Pauli operators, such as $X'_i$.  We will additionally add a third set of $n$ ancilla qubits written with two primes; these will be added solely to ensure that the Hamiltonian we write is redundancy-free; the only terms in the Hamiltonian involving these ancilla qubits will be those in \cref{combineacg} or \cref{combineacgAF} below.
These two anticommutation glasses
may have different coupling constants $J,J'$.

We now add an interaction term to the Hamiltonian.
For a ferromagnetic interaction, take
\be
\label{combineacg}
H_{\rm coupling}
=-\widetilde J\sum_i
\left(X_iX_i'X_i''+D_iD_i'Z_i''\right),
\ee
where $\widetilde J\geq0$.
 If both anticommutation glasses are in the same type of state (either $X$-type or $Z$-type), then the
system can increase the log of its partition function by roughly $n \ln(\cosh(\beta \tilde J))$ as one can gain from thermal fluctuations in either the $X$ or $Z$ terms in $H_{\rm coupling}$, while little increase in log partition function is possible if the glasses are in different types of state.  Thus, such a term acts a ferromagnetic coupling.

Similarly, we can induce an effective anti-ferromagnetic coupling with
\be
\label{combineacgAF}
H_{\rm coupling}
=-\widetilde J\sum_i
\left(X_iD_i'X_i''+D_iX_i'Z_i''\right).
\ee
Now an interaction term agrees with the chosen families at both endpoints precisely when the two choices are different.

The ancillas are redundancy-free even though the two interaction types use the same ancilla. In any binary relation, its $X$ and $Z$ coordinates force the coefficients of both terms using that ancilla to vanish. Repeating this leaves only relations inside the original glasses, which do not occur because their matrices are invertible.

By combining then many anticommutation glasses, we can build an arbitrary effective Ising spin system.  We keep the degree of the interaction graph for this Ising spin system bounded by a constant, so that the degree of the anticommutation graph is only increased by a constant factor.
Note that the increase in log-partition function is roughly, for small $\beta \tilde J$, equal to $\frac{1}{2}n \beta^2 \tilde J^2$, and hence is proportional to $\kappa \tilde J^2$.  Thus, while our effective Ising spin system will be effectively at nonzero temperature, at large enough $\kappa$ we can take the temperature low enough that approximating the partition function is NP-hard.

In the construction above, we have been treating the $X$-type and $Z$-type states as some effective classical states.  Likely there is some effective quantum tunneling between these states, with that tunneling becoming small at large $\kappa$.  Thus, it might be possible to encode effective quantum Ising Hamiltonians using anticommutation glasses.  We leave this as an open question.

In the next three subsections, we justify the claims about the behavior of the anticommutation glass. Then, in \cref{sec:NP_formal}, we complete our argument with a formal NP-hardness proof.

\subsection{Series Expansion}
\label{subsec:se}
First consider small $\kappa$.  We series expand the expectation values $\langle O_i \rangle$ in $\beta$.  Consider a $Z$-type operator $O_i$.
We have
\begin{eqnarray}
\langle O_i \rangle &=& \tr(\exp(-\beta H))^{-1} \tr(\exp(-\beta H) O_i) \\ \nonumber
&=& \bigl(1+\frac{1}{2}\beta^2 n (1+J^2)+\ldots\bigr)^{-1} \bigl(\beta J+\frac{n-1}{2} \beta^3 J^3+\frac{1}{3!} \beta^3 J^3 +\frac{1}{3!} \beta^3 J \sum_{j=1}^n \tr(O_i O_j O_j O_i + O_i O_j O_i O_j + O_i O_i O_j O_j)+\ldots \bigr)
\end{eqnarray}
In the second line, the term $\frac{n-1}{2} \beta^3 J^3$ arises from expanding terms in $\exp(\beta H)$ where $O_i$ appears once and some different $Z$-type operator appears twice.
The trace $\tr(O_i O_j O_j O_i + O_i O_j O_i O_j + O_i O_i O_j O_j)$ will equal either $3$ or $1$ depending on whether $O_i,O_j$ commute or anticommute.
Summing this trace over $j$, since both cases are roughly equally likely, we get roughly $2n$.
Hence, we have
\begin{eqnarray}
\langle O_i \rangle & \approx& \bigl(1+\frac{1}{2}\beta^2 n (1+J^2)+\ldots\bigr)^{-1} \bigl(\beta J+\frac{n-1}{2} \beta^3 J^3+\frac{1}{3!} \beta^3 J^3 +\frac{1}{3!} \beta^3 J (2n)+\ldots \bigr) \\ \nonumber
&=&
\beta J-\frac{1}{3} \beta^3 J^3-\frac{1}{6} \beta^3 J n+\ldots
\end{eqnarray}
The terms $\beta J-(1/3)\beta^3J^3$ are the first two terms in the expansion of $\tanh(\beta J)$. The additional term arises from anticommutation. More precisely, if $k_i$ is the number of $X$-type generators anticommuting with $O_i$, the trace sum above is $3n-2k_i$, and for fixed finite $n$,
\be
\langle O_i\rangle
=\beta J-\frac13\beta^3J^3-\frac13\beta^3Jk_i+O(\beta^5).
\ee
For a dense row with $k_i\simeq n/2$, this gives the displayed approximation. Since $\kappa=\beta^2d$, the anticommutation correction is $-(k_i/3d)\beta J\kappa$, which is of order $\beta J\kappa$ when $k_i=\Theta(d)$. The term $-(1/3)\beta^3J^3$ is smaller at large $n$ and fixed $\kappa$.

This series expansion gives the claimed decrease in ratio for both $X$-type and $Z$-type operators; we have done the calculation only for $Z$-type but we see a decrease for both $J<1$ and $J>1$.

We could proceed to higher orders in the series expansion to explore larger $\beta$, but instead we use numerical simulation.

\subsection{Numerical Simulation}
\label{subsec:ns}
With the negative signs in $H_A(J)$, the Hamiltonian has no sign problem in the $Z$ basis.
We performed a path integral Monte Carlo.  We used $50$ time slices (more slices may improve the simulation).  

We started with all physical spins on all time slices intially equal to $+1$, so that each $D_i$ initially equals $+1$ and each $Z$-type Hamiltonian contribution $-JD_i$ equals $-J$.
We included Monte Carlo moves that flip a spin at a single time slice, and also
we added a type of nonlocal move to the simulation.  For each $i$, this move flips spins in the $Z$ basis so that the sign of operator the $Z$-type operator $O_{i+n}$ flips in expectation, but all other $Z$-type operators do not have their expectation value change.  We do this flip at \emph{all} time slices so that also the expectation value of the $X$-type operators does not change.  Indeed, for simplicity, we do not explicitly do this move by flipping spins, but rather we consider a Monte Carlo move that leaves the spin unchanged but flips the sign of the coefficient in front of the $Z$-type operators.

This move helps equilibration; if this move is not done, then it is very hard for the system to equilibrate the $Z$-type operators.  Indeed, for the given initial configuration, a single spin flip would change the sign of roughly half the $Z$-type operators, costing a large amount of energy, so that the move is likely to be rejected even at fairly high temperatures.  With this nonlocal move, we can flip a single operator at a time, giving correct behavior at high temperature.

We report simulations below on a single random instance for $n=50,100,200$.  Similar behavior was shown for other random instances.  However, we show in some cases multiple runs for the same instance to show issues with equilibrating the Monte Carlo.
We did $5\times 10^6$ Monte Carlo steps before measuring observables, and then did $5\times 10^6$ Monte Carlo steps to measure observables, averaging data over those steps.

In \cref{fig1} we plot the ratio for the sum of $Z$-type operators for $J=0.9,1.1$ for $n=50,100,200$ as a function of inverse temperature $\beta$.  The $x$-axis is scaled by a factor proportional to $1/\sqrt{n}$, and we see a good collapse of the curves for $n=50,100,200$ on top of each other for given $J$.
However, we observe one surprising effect: the $J=0.9,n=200$ ratio follows the $J=0.9,n=50,100$ ratios at smaller $\beta$, but then at large $\beta$ it jumps up close to $1$.  We believe that this is a result of a failure to equilibrate the quantum Monte Carlo simulation: the initial configuration is well-polarized in the $Z$-direction and while equilibrium should have to a smaller expectation value of $Z$-type operators combined with a larger expectation value of $X$-type operators (which in this kind of quantum Monte Carlo simulation translates to a larger  \emph{entropy} at the cost of a higher \emph{energy}), the system can get stuck in the $Z$-polarized basin and be unable to explore the full space.

\begin{figure}

\caption{Plot of ratio against inverse temperature for $J=0.9,1.1$, and $n=50,100,200$.  Inverse temperature is multiplied by a scale factor, given in parenthesis in the legend.  The scale factor is proportional to $1/\sqrt{n}$.}
\label{fig1}
\end{figure}

In \cref{fig2}, we plot multiple independent Monte Carlo runs for $J=0.9,n=200$.  We see that this jump in ratio appears in all of them, but it appears at slightly different inverse temperature in different ones.  In sum cases the ratio does not jump fully to $1$; this may be due to the system leaving the $Z$-polarized basin partway through the simulation, as observables are plotted averaged over the run after the initial warmup.

\begin{figure}

\caption{Plot of ratio against inverse temperature for $J=0.9,n=200$.  Multiple Monte Carlo runs with the same random realization.}
\label{fig2}
\end{figure}

In \cref{fig3}, we show that failure to equilibrate can occur even for $J>1$ for this initial condition.  We report multiple independent Monte Carlo runs with the same random realization.  All but one of the runs follow a smooth curve, but one run has the ratio jump to a smaller value for one value of inverse temperature, so failure to equilibrate is much less frequent.  

\begin{figure}

\caption{Plot of ratio against inverse temperature for $J=1.1,n=100$.  Multiple Monte Carlo runs with the same random realization.}
\label{fig3}
\end{figure}

We leave it as an open problem to improve the equilibration of the Monte Carlo in this kind of model.  We did simulations independently at different temperature.  Using a simulation at a given temperature as a warmup for a nearby temperature may help.  Alternatively, one may try parallel tempering in both temperature and $J$.

\subsection{Toy Model}
\label{subsec:tm}
Finally, we consider the behavior in a toy analytic model. 
Let us first neglect the $X$-type terms and consider only $Z$-type terms.
Consider a typical thermal state in the $Z$-basis of this Hamiltonian with only $Z$-type terms, and call this state $|0\rangle$.  Let $E=\langle0|-J\sum_iD_i|0\rangle$ be the energy of the $Z$-type part in this state. Its thermal mean is $-nJ\tanh(\beta J)$, so typically $E\simeq-\beta J^2n$ when $\beta J\ll1$ and $\beta J\sqrt n\gg1$. Since $d=\Theta(n)$, its magnitude is then of order $J^2\sqrt\kappa\sqrt n$.

We now consider the $X$-type terms. For $i=1,\ldots,n$, let $|i\rangle=O_i |0\rangle$.  
Each of these states has some expectation value for the sum of $Z$-type operators, but since a single spin flip will typically flip about half of the $Z$-type terms, this expectation value can be approximated by just considering the expectation value in a random state, and hence the expectation value is typically proportional to $\sqrt{n}$.  Hence, for large enough $\kappa$, this expectation value is much smaller than $-E$ for most of these states $|i\rangle$.
Hence, considering just the states $|0\rangle,|i\rangle$, we have a Hamiltonian with $n+1$ basis states, a diagonal term on $|0\rangle$ that is proportional to $-\beta J^2 n$ and hopping to all the other basis states $|i\rangle$ with hopping proportional to $1$.  Ignoring the diagonal terms on those states $|i\rangle$, we can consider only the two basis states $|0\rangle$ and $(1/\sqrt{n}) \sum_i |i\rangle$, with hopping proportional to $\sqrt{n}$ between them.

Thus the collective hopping has magnitude $\sqrt n$, but is small compared with the diagonal splitting $|E|$: their ratio is of order $1/(\beta J^2\sqrt n)$, and hence of order $\kappa^{-1/2}$ for fixed $J>0$ and $d=\Theta(n)$.
\subsection{Formal proof}
\label{sec:NP_formal}

Place an anticommutation glass at each vertex of a cubic graph, using the interaction \cref{combineacgAF} with $J=1$. Choosing one of the two operator families at each vertex defines a cut. We will show that the log partition function records the size of a maximum cut, up to a bounded error per vertex. Keeping a commuting subset of the Hamiltonian gives the lower bound; the upper bound comes from the thermal series.

\subsubsection{Construction}

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a simple cubic graph, with $N=|V|$ vertices and $M=|E|=3N/2$ edges. At each vertex $v$, place a block of $n$ qubits with Hamiltonian
\be
D_{v,i}=\prod_{j=1}^n Z_{v,j}^{(A_v)_{ij}},
\qquad H_v=-\sum_{i=1}^n(X_{v,i}+D_{v,i}),
\label{eq:glass_block}
\ee
where the invertible binary matrix $A_v$ will be chosen below. Thus each block has two commuting families, which we label $X$ and $Z$; the label $Z$ refers to the operators $D_{v,i}$.

For each edge $e=uv$, choose an orientation to fix the order of $u,v$, introduce $n$ ancilla qubits $c_{e,i}$, and add
\be
V_e=-\widetilde J\sum_{i=1}^n
\left(X_{u,i}D_{v,i}X_{c_{e,i}}+D_{u,i}X_{v,i}Z_{c_{e,i}}\right),
\qquad
H_G=\sum_{v\in V}H_v+\sum_{e\in E}V_e.
\label{eq:glass_coupling}
\ee
The two terms in parentheses give the two interaction types $(X,Z)$ and $(Z,X)$. If the endpoint blocks choose different families, one term agrees with both choices. If they choose the same family, neither term does.

For the matrices, take $n=4^s\geq16$ and label the qubits and generators within each block by $\mathbb F=\mathbb F_n$. Properly color $G$ with four colors, which is possible by a greedy coloring since the maximum degree is three. Assign a distinct nonzero field element to each color, and let $\alpha_v$ be the element assigned to the color of $v$. Set
\be
A_v(x,y)=\delta_{xy}+\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbb F}(\alpha_vxy),
\label{eq:glass_matrices}
\ee
where $\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbb F}$ is the field trace to $\mathbb F_2$ and addition is over $\mathbb F_2$. These matrices are symmetric and satisfy $A_v^2=I$. Every row with nonzero label has weight $n/2\pm1$. The four choices give blocks related by permutations of their qubits and generators. The sign estimates are proved in Appendix~\ref{app:glass_finite}.

The ancillas ensure that adding the couplings has not introduced relations. Indeed, in any product proportional to the identity, the $X$ and $Z$ exponents on each ancilla must both be even. Hence every coupling term occurs an even number of times. The remaining onsite generators are independent because each $A_v$ is invertible. Thus $H_G$ is redundancy-free.

An onsite term has at most $4n$ anticommuting neighbors. An edge term has at most $2n$ such neighbors among onsite terms, $4n$ on other incident edges, and $n$ in the other term of its own edge. On the other hand, a nonzero-labeled row of any $A_v$ already gives at least $n/2-1$ anticommuting neighbors. Therefore the maximum anticommutation degree satisfies
\be
\frac n2-1\leq d\leq7n.
\label{eq:glass_degree}
\ee

Fix positive integers $t,b$ with $b\geq t$, and set
\be
\beta=\frac b{\sqrt n},
\qquad \widetilde J=\frac tb,
\qquad K=\frac{t^2}{2}.
\label{eq:glass_scales}
\ee
All coefficients then have magnitude at most one, and $\beta=\Theta(d^{-1/2})$ for fixed $b$. The quantity $K$ will be the gain in the log partition function from one cut edge. We use the normalized partition function
\be
\mathcal Z_{n,G}=2^{-n(N+M)}\operatorname{Tr}e^{-\beta H_G}.
\label{eq:glass_normalization}
\ee
\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:glass_hardness}
There are positive integers $t\leq b$ and $n_0$ with the following property. Fix any power of four $n\geq n_0$. Then, given a cubic graph $G$ on $N$ vertices, approximating $\mathcal Z_{n,G}(\beta)$ within a factor of two is NP-hard at inverse temperature $\beta=\Theta(d^{-1/2})$, with constants independent of $G$. The corresponding Hamiltonians $H_G$ are redundancy-free, have coefficients of magnitude at most one, and satisfy \cref{eq:glass_degree}. The block size $n$ is independent of the graph size $N$.
\end{theorem}

\subsubsection{A commuting lower bound}

First choose a family $\sigma_v\in\{X,Z\}$ at each vertex. Retain only that onsite family. On every edge whose endpoints choose different families, also retain the one coupling term agreeing with both choices. Remove all other terms.

Every retained term commutes with every other. At a block, all retained operators belong to the same commuting family, and each ancilla occurs in at most one retained term. The retained generators are also independent, since they form a subset of the original generators. Consequently their normalized partition function is a product of coshes:
\be
\mathcal Z_\sigma=(\cosh\beta)^{nN}
(\cosh(\beta\widetilde J))^{n\operatorname{cut}_G(\sigma)},
\label{eq:glass_commuting_partition}
\ee
where $\operatorname{cut}_G(\sigma)$ counts edges with different endpoint labels. To see the factorization, expand each exponential as $e^{hP}=\cosh h+P\sinh h$. Independence makes the trace of every nonempty product of distinct generators vanish, leaving only the product of coshes.

Removing a term from a redundancy-free Hamiltonian cannot increase its partition function. Indeed, fix all other couplings and write $g(x)=\log\operatorname{Tr}e^{Q+xP}$, where $P$ is the term being removed. Every word with an odd number of appearances of $P$ has zero trace, so $g$ is even. It is also convex, and hence $g(x)\geq g(0)$. Applying this one term at a time, and choosing a maximum cut, gives
\be
\log\mathcal Z_{n,G}\geq
nN\log\cosh\left(\frac b{\sqrt n}\right)
+n\log\cosh\left(\frac t{\sqrt n}\right)\operatorname{MAXCUT}(G).
\label{eq:glass_lower_exact}
\ee
To bound the finite-$n$ error, use
\be
0\leq\frac{x^2}{2}-\log\cosh x\leq\frac{x^4}{12}.
\label{eq:glass_cosh_bound}
\ee
